        {
          "id": "def-fdmath-finite-ensemble-and-covariance",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Finite weighted ensemble and covariance",
          "reference": "items/def-fdmath-finite-ensemble-and-covariance.md",
          "design_state": "Step 1 complete draft; readiness recorded separately.",
          "dependency_level": 0,
          "deps": [],
          "dependency_roles": {},
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $N\\ge1$ be an integer and let $a_1,\\ldots,a_N\\in[0,\\infty)$ satisfy $\\sum_{j=1}^N a_j=1$. The fixed tuple $(a_1,\\ldots,a_N)$ is a finite ensemble of weights. For integers $m,n\\ge1$, let $X=(X_1,\\ldots,X_N)\\in(\\mathbb R^m)^N$ and $Y=(Y_1,\\ldots,Y_N)\\in(\\mathbb R^n)^N$. Define the weighted mean of $X$ and its $j$th fluctuation by\n\n$$\\bar X:=\\sum_{j=1}^N a_jX_j,\\qquad X'_j:=X_j-\\bar X.$$\n\nUsing the same weights, define the cross-covariance tensor\n\n$$C(X,Y):=\\sum_{j=1}^N a_j X'_j\\otimes Y'_j\\in\\mathbb R^{m\\times n},$$\n\nwhere $x\\otimes y$ is the matrix with entries $(x\\otimes y)_{rs}=x_r y_s$. The covariance tensor of $X$ is $C(X):=C(X,X)$. If $D$ is a set and $X_j:D\\to\\mathbb R^m$, $Y_j:D\\to\\mathbb R^n$ are functions, apply these formulas pointwise at each $q\\in D$ using the same fixed weights. No probability space, limiting operation, or interpretation as an empirical sample is part of this definition.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Glenn Flierl and Raffaele Ferrari, MIT OpenCourseWare 12.820, Turbulence in the Ocean and Atmosphere, Chapter 3 (Spring 2006 lecture notes)",
                "url": "https://www.ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-820-turbulence-in-the-ocean-and-atmosphere-spring-2006/a3ea9a7900aee9c9ebaf444ab2d92c1a_ch3.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Gregory L. Eyink, Johns Hopkins University Turbulence Notes, Chapter VIb, §(B) Reynolds Averaging and kinetic-energy balances, pp. 17–19",
                "url": "https://www.ams.jhu.edu/~eyink/Turbulence/notes/ChapterVIb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
          "library": "mathematics"
        },
        {
          "id": "thm-fdmath-finite-reynolds-identity",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Exact finite Reynolds averaged equation",
          "reference": "items/thm-fdmath-finite-reynolds-identity.md",
          "design_state": "Step 1 complete draft; readiness recorded separately.",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-fdmath-finite-ensemble-and-covariance"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-fdmath-finite-ensemble-and-covariance": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval, let $d,N\\ge1$ be integers, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be open, and fix constants $a_j\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{j=1}^N a_j=1$ and a common $\\nu\\ge0$. For $j=1,\\ldots,N$, let $u_j:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be continuously differentiable in $(t,x)$ with continuous second spatial derivatives, let $p_j:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuously differentiable, and let $f_j:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be continuous. Suppose pointwise that\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}u_j=0,\\qquad \\partial_tu_j+\\operatorname{div}(u_j\\otimes u_j)+\\nabla p_j=\\nu\\Delta u_j+f_j.$$\n\nHere $(u\\otimes v)_{rs}=u_rv_s$, $\\operatorname{div}$ of a matrix field is rowwise, $(\\operatorname{div}A)_r=\\sum_{s=1}^d\\partial_{x_s}A_{rs}$, and $\\Delta$ acts componentwise. Define the weighted means $\\bar u=\\sum_j a_ju_j$, $\\bar p=\\sum_j a_jp_j$, and $\\bar f=\\sum_j a_jf_j$, the fluctuations $v_j=u_j-\\bar u$, and the covariance tensor $R=\\sum_j a_jv_j\\otimes v_j$. Then, pointwise on $I\\times\\Omega$,\n\n$$\\operatorname{div}\\bar u=0,\\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^N a_jv_j=0,\\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^N a_j u_j\\otimes u_j=\\bar u\\otimes\\bar u+R,$$\n\nand the exact averaged equation is\n\n$$\\partial_t\\bar u+\\operatorname{div}(\\bar u\\otimes\\bar u)+\\nabla\\bar p=\\nu\\Delta\\bar u+\\bar f-\\operatorname{div}R.$$\n\nThe tensor $R$ is symmetric positive semidefinite, with $z^{\\mathsf T}Rz=\\sum_j a_j(v_j\\cdot z)^2\\ge0$ for every $z\\in\\mathbb R^d$. With $\\operatorname{tr}A:=\\sum_{r=1}^d A_{rr}$ for $A\\in\\mathbb R^{d\\times d}$, the pointwise energy split is $\\sum_j a_j|u_j|^2=|\\bar u|^2+\\operatorname{tr}R$. These are identities for the specified smooth functions; they assert neither existence of such solutions nor a statistical or physical interpretation.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Glenn Flierl and Raffaele Ferrari, MIT OpenCourseWare 12.820, Turbulence in the Ocean and Atmosphere, Chapter 3 (Spring 2006 lecture notes)",
                "url": "https://www.ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-820-turbulence-in-the-ocean-and-atmosphere-spring-2006/a3ea9a7900aee9c9ebaf444ab2d92c1a_ch3.pdf"
              },
              {
                "title": "Gregory L. Eyink, Johns Hopkins University Turbulence Notes, Chapter VIb, §(B) Reynolds Averaging and kinetic-energy balances, pp. 17–19",
                "url": "https://www.ams.jhu.edu/~eyink/Turbulence/notes/ChapterVIb.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
