          "physical_scope": "Conditional worked calculation for a smooth positive-density material motion and a specified local-equilibrium compressible Newtonian/Fourier model branch. It derives a model entropy-production identity from adopted balances and constitutive assumptions; it is not a measurement or a claim of universal material response.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $I$ be an open time interval containing $0$, with time measured in seconds; let $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded $C^1$ reference body, and let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be an open neighborhood of $\\overline B$. Let $\\alpha\\in C^2(I,(0,\\infty))$ be dimensionless with $\\alpha(0)=1$, so $\\alpha'$ is measured in s$^{-1}$, and define $\\chi:I\\times U\\to\\mathbb R^3$ by $\\chi(t,a)=\\alpha(t)a$. This is a $C^2$ orientation-preserving motion with $\\chi(0,a)=a$ and positive reference density $\\rho_0\\in C^1(\\overline B)$ in kg m$^{-3}$. Assume the adopted smooth material mass, linear-momentum, total-energy, and entropy balances on every bounded $C^1$ material subregion. On the example's local-equilibrium branch, $e=e(v,s)\\in C^2$ on an open state domain, with specific volume $v=1/\\rho$ in m$^3$ kg$^{-1}$, $T=e_s>0$ in K, $p=-e_v$ in Pa, and $de=T\\,ds-p\\,dv$. Adopt the specified symmetric Newtonian stress and Fourier heat-flux closure\n\n$$\\sigma=-pI_3+\\tau,\\qquad \\tau=2\\mu D_0+\\zeta\\theta I_3,\\qquad q=-\\kappa_T\\nabla T,$$\n\nwith constant coefficients $\\mu,\\zeta,\\kappa_T\\ge0$ in Pa s, Pa s, and W m$^{-1}$ K$^{-1}$, respectively. Assume $\\rho,u,e,T,s,q,\\sigma\\in C^1$ and $b,r\\in C^0$ on the relevant swept regions, with $\\rho,T$ positive. Then $F=\\alpha I_3$, $J=\\alpha^3$, $u(t,x)=(\\alpha'(t)/\\alpha(t))x$, and $\\theta=3\\alpha'/\\alpha$, so $D_0=0$ while the mass theorem gives $\\rho(t,\\chi(t,a))=\\rho_0(a)/\\alpha(t)^3$. The local entropy-production residual $g=\\rho D_ts+\\operatorname{div}(q/T)-\\rho r/T$ is\n\n$$g=\\frac{\\zeta\\theta^2}{T}+\\frac{\\kappa_T|\\nabla T|^2}{T^2}\\ge0.$$\n\nIn the spatially uniform-temperature case, the heat-conduction term vanishes and the bulk-viscous production is $\\zeta\\theta^2/T$. The residual has units W m$^{-3}$ K$^{-1}$. This is a conditional deduction for the stated closure and balances, not a claim that actual fluids universally obey them.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-generated",
            "proof": "ai-generated"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Ain A. Sonin, Fundamental Laws of Motion for Particles, Material Volumes, and Control Volumes, MIT 2.25 lecture notes",
