          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\tau>0$ s, $I=(-\\tau,\\tau)$, and use initial and current Cartesian coordinates measured in metres. Let $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be a nonempty bounded open reference region with $C^1$ boundary and let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be an open neighborhood of $\\overline B$. Choose $e\\in\\mathbb R^3$ with Euclidean norm $|e|=1$ and a speed $c>0$ measured in m s$^{-1}$. Define $\\chi_1,\\chi_2:I\\times U\\to\\mathbb R^3$ by $\\chi_1(t,a)=a+ct e$ and $\\chi_2(t,a)=a-ct e$. On the open spatial region $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^3$, define $u_1,u_2:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^3$ by $u_1(t,x)=ce$ and $u_2(t,x)=-ce$. Take equal dimensionless weights $a_1=a_2=1/2$, zero pressure-per-unit-mass fields $p_j:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ (units m$^2$ s$^{-2}$), zero body-force-per-unit-mass fields $f_j:I\\times\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R^3$ (units m s$^{-2}$), and common kinematic viscosity $\\nu=0$ m$^2$ s$^{-1}$ in the per-unit-mass finite Reynolds identity. For the formal constant-density interpretation, adopt a constant density parameter $\\rho_0>0$ in kg m$^{-3}$ for momentum scaling. Define the covariance $R=\\sum_{j=1}^2a_j(u_j-\\bar u)\\otimes(u_j-\\bar u)$ and define the effective Reynolds-stress correction $\\sigma_R=-\\rho_0R$ when the momentum equation is written with a $+\\operatorname{div}\\sigma$ contribution on the right. Then $\\bar u=0$, $R=c^2e\\otimes e$, and $\\rho_0R$ is a nonzero positive-semidefinite tensor with units Pa, while $\\sigma_R$ has the opposite sign. This is an exact finite-field illustration, not an empirical observation or a statistical turbulence model.",
