          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $\\rho_0,\\mu>0$, $\\nu=\\mu/\\rho_0$, and $U\\in\\mathbb R$. Use the constant-density incompressible model\n\n$$\\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u+\\rho_0^{-1}\\nabla p-\\nu\\Delta u=0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}u=0,$$\n\nwith Cartesian derivatives and componentwise Laplacian; its unsteady Stokes system omits the convective term. Set $I=(0,\\infty)$ and $\\Omega_+=\\mathbb R\\times(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$, with coordinates $(x,y,z)$. Define $I_0=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-r^2}\\,dr$ and $E(s)=I_0^{-1}\\int_s^\\infty e^{-r^2}\\,dr$ for $s\\ge0$. On $I\\times\\Omega_+$ define $u:I\\times\\Omega_+\\to\\mathbb R^3$ by $u(t,x,y,z)=(U E(y/(2\\sqrt{\\nu t})),0,0)$ and let $p$ be any constant scalar field. The field is smooth for $t>0,y>0$ and satisfies the pointwise unsteady Stokes equation $\\partial_tu+\\rho_0^{-1}\\nabla p-\\nu\\Delta u=0$ and $\\operatorname{div}u=0$. It also solves the full constant-density Navier–Stokes system because its convective term vanishes. For each fixed $t>0$ and $x,z\\in\\mathbb R$, its one-sided boundary limit is $\\lim_{y\\downarrow0}u(t,x,y,z)=(U,0,0)$ and its limit as $y\\to\\infty$ is $0$; for each fixed $x,z\\in\\mathbb R$ and $y>0$, its limit as $t\\downarrow0$ is $0$. The corner $(t,y)=(0,0)$ is excluded from the classical claim. For every $x,z\\in\\mathbb R$, $t>0$, $0<\\varepsilon<1$, and $y\\ge2\\sqrt{\\nu t}\\sqrt{\\log(1/\\varepsilon)}$, the absolute value of the first velocity component satisfies $|U E(y/(2\\sqrt{\\nu t}))|\\le\\varepsilon|U|$.\n\nSeparately, let $\\mathbb T_L=\\mathbb R/(L\\mathbb Z)$ for $L>0$, set $\\mathbb T^3=\\mathbb T_{L_x}\\times\\mathbb T_{L_y}\\times\\mathbb T_{L_z}$, and let $\\pi:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb T^3$ be the quotient map, where $L_x,L_y,L_z>0$. Equip this quotient with the flat smooth structure and metric induced by Euclidean translations, identifying each tangent space with $\\mathbb R^3$ through the derivative of $\\pi$. In this branch, the Navier–Stokes equations mean that the periodic lift satisfies the displayed constant-density system pointwise on $\\mathbb R^3$. Choose an integer $m\\ge1$, put $k=2\\pi m/L_y$, and let $A\\in\\mathbb R$. The periodic lift $\\widetilde u(t,x,y,z)=(A e^{-\\nu k^2t}\\sin(ky),0,0)$ defines a smooth vector field $u$ on $\\mathbb T^3$ by $u(t,\\pi(q))=d\\pi_q(\\widetilde u(t,q))$; with constant pressure, it is an exact Navier–Stokes solution for all $t\\in\\mathbb R$. Choose $c,b>0$ with $c<L_x/2$, $b<L_y/2$, and $0<\\alpha:=kb<\\pi$. Define the closed lifted loop and its quotient by\n\n$$\\widetilde X(t,s)=\\left(c\\sin(2\\pi s)+\\frac{A}{\\nu k^2}(1-e^{-\\nu k^2t})\\sin(kb\\cos(2\\pi s)),\\,b\\cos(2\\pi s),\\,0\\right),\\qquad X(t,s)=\\pi(\\widetilde X(t,s)),\\quad t\\in\\mathbb R,\\quad 0\\le s\\le1.$$\n\nThe displayed lift satisfies $\\widetilde X(t,0)=\\widetilde X(t,1)$. The loop is material when $\\partial_t\\widetilde X(t,s)=\\widetilde u(t,\\widetilde X(t,s))$, as it does here. Define its flat-torus circulation by $\\Gamma(t)=\\int_0^1\\widetilde u(t,\\widetilde X(t,s))\\cdot\\partial_s\\widetilde X(t,s)\\,ds$; this value is unchanged if the lift is translated by a vector in $L_x\\mathbb Z\\times L_y\\mathbb Z\\times L_z\\mathbb Z$. Let $C_\\alpha=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos\\theta\\,\\sin(\\alpha\\cos\\theta)\\,d\\theta$. Then $C_\\alpha>0$ and $\\Gamma(t)=AcC_\\alpha e^{-\\nu k^2t}$, which is nonconstant when $A\\ne0$. The corner-excluded wall-layer solution and periodic material-loop solution are distinct exact mathematical models; neither states an experiment or a general boundary-layer theorem. Under SI interpretation, $x,y,z,L_x,L_y,L_z,b,c$ are in metres, $t$ in seconds, $u,U,A$ in metres per second, $\\rho_0$ in kilograms per cubic metre, $\\mu$ in pascal-seconds, $\\nu$ in square metres per second, and $k$ in inverse metres.",
