          "id": "thm-fd-x07",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Bounded-domain Stokes residual estimate",
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $n\\ge2$ and let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded $C^1$ domain, meaning a bounded connected open set whose boundary is locally a $C^1$ graph and which lies on one side of that graph after a rigid change of coordinates. Let $\\mu>0$, $w\\in C^2(\\overline\\Omega;\\mathbb R^n)$, $q\\in C^1(\\overline\\Omega)$, and $f\\in C(\\overline\\Omega;\\mathbb R^n)$; $C^k(\\overline\\Omega)$ means that derivatives through order $k$ extend continuously to the closure. For ordinary Euclidean coordinates $x_1,\\ldots,x_n$, set $\\partial_j=\\partial/\\partial x_j$, define $\\nabla r=(\\partial_1r,\\ldots,\\partial_nr)$ for scalar $r$, define $\\nabla\\cdot v=\\sum_{i=1}^n\\partial_i v_i$ and $(\\nabla v)_{ij}=\\partial_jv_i$ for vector $v$, define $(v\\cdot\\nabla)v=\\sum_{j=1}^n v_j\\partial_jv$, and define the vector Laplacian by $(\\Delta v)_i=\\sum_{j=1}^n\\partial_j^2v_i$. For $a=(a_i)_{i=1}^n\\in\\mathbb R^n$ set $|a|=(\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2)^{1/2}$, so $|\\nabla v|^2=\\sum_{i,j=1}^n|\\partial_jv_i|^2$. For vector fields $g,v$, write $\\|g\\|_2=(\\int_\\Omega|g|^2\\,dx)^{1/2}$ and $\\|\\nabla v\\|_2=(\\int_\\Omega\\sum_{i,j=1}^n|\\partial_jv_i|^2\\,dx)^{1/2}$. Suppose pointwise that $\\nabla\\cdot w=0$, $w|_{\\partial\\Omega}=0$, and $-\\mu\\Delta w+\\nabla q=f$. Also assume that a stated constant $C_\\Omega>0$ satisfies the zero-trace Poincare inequality $\\|v\\|_2\\le C_\\Omega\\|\\nabla v\\|_2$ for every $v\\in C^1(\\overline\\Omega;\\mathbb R^n)$ with $v|_{\\partial\\Omega}=0$. Then\n\n$$\\|\\nabla w\\|_2\\le \\frac{C_\\Omega}{\\mu}\\|f\\|_2,\\qquad \\|w\\|_2\\le \\frac{C_\\Omega^2}{\\mu}\\|f\\|_2.$$\n\nFor $L_x,L_z,h>0$, set $\\mathbb T_L=\\mathbb R/(L\\mathbb Z)$ for $L>0$ and $Q=\\mathbb T_{L_x}\\times(0,h)\\times\\mathbb T_{L_z}$, the periodic channel cell. A field on $\\overline Q$ is identified with its lift to $\\mathbb R\\times[0,h]\\times\\mathbb R$ periodic with periods $L_x,L_z$ in the first and third coordinates. Integrals on $Q$ use Lebesgue measure on one fundamental cell in each periodic coordinate; $\\|v\\|_{L^2(Q)}^2=\\int_Q|v|^2$ and $\\|\\nabla v\\|_{L^2(Q)}^2=\\int_Q\\sum_{i,j=1}^3|\\partial_jv_i|^2$. Every $v\\in C^1(\\overline Q;\\mathbb R^3)$ periodic in the two torus variables and zero on $y=0,h$ satisfies $\\|v\\|_{L^2(Q)}\\le h\\|\\nabla v\\|_{L^2(Q)}$. This channel inequality is a separate direct estimate on the periodic cell; it does not treat that cell as a bounded $C^1$ Euclidean domain.\n\nIn dimension three, let $\\rho_0>0$, $\\nu=\\mu/\\rho_0$, and suppose already-existing time-independent fields $u_{\\rm NS},u_{\\rm S}\\in C^2(\\overline\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)$ and $p_{\\rm NS},p_{\\rm S}\\in C^1(\\overline\\Omega)$ are divergence-free, have the same boundary velocity, and satisfy the constant-density incompressible Navier-Stokes and Stokes equations $\\rho_0(u_{\\rm NS}\\cdot\\nabla)u_{\\rm NS}+\\nabla p_{\\rm NS}-\\mu\\Delta u_{\\rm NS}=0$ and $\\nabla p_{\\rm S}-\\mu\\Delta u_{\\rm S}=0$. If $f=-\\rho_0(u_{\\rm NS}\\cdot\\nabla)u_{\\rm NS}$, then the preceding estimate applies to $w=u_{\\rm NS}-u_{\\rm S}$ and $q=p_{\\rm NS}-p_{\\rm S}$ whenever the stated Poincare bound holds. This is an a-posteriori residual estimate for already-given fields; it asserts no solution existence, no exterior-domain bound, and no error bound from Reynolds number alone. The constant $C_\\Omega=h$ established for the periodic channel is not transferred to an exterior domain.",
