          "id": "thm-fd-x10",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Conditional nonlinear Couette energy stability",
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $L_x,L_z,h,\\nu,\\rho_0>0$, $S\\in\\mathbb R$, and $T>0$. For $L>0$, define $\\mathbb T_L=\\mathbb R/(L\\mathbb Z)$ and set $Q=\\mathbb T_{L_x}\\times(0,h)\\times\\mathbb T_{L_z}$, with Lebesgue integration over one fundamental cell. Define $\\mu:=\\rho_0\\nu>0$, so $\\nu=\\mu/\\rho_0$ as in the formal convention of definition def-fd-x00. Let $U_b(x,y,z)=(Sy,0,0)$. Let $u\\in C^1([0,T];C^2(\\overline Q;\\mathbb R^3))$ and $p\\in C([0,T];C^1(\\overline Q))$, periodic in $x,z$. Regard these fields as periodic lifts $\\widetilde u:[0,T]\\times\\mathbb R\\times[0,h]\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and $\\widetilde p:[0,T]\\times\\mathbb R\\times[0,h]\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$, with periods $L_x,L_z$ in $x,z$; all spatial derivatives and equations are interpreted on these lifts. For $a=(a_1,a_2,a_3)\\in\\mathbb R^3$, use the Euclidean norm $|a|=(a_1^2+a_2^2+a_3^2)^{1/2}$ as in the norm convention in theorem thm-fd-x07. With ordinary Cartesian derivatives, $\\nabla p=(\\partial_xp,\\partial_yp,\\partial_zp)$, $\\nabla\\cdot u=\\partial_xu_1+\\partial_yu_2+\\partial_zu_3$, $(u\\cdot\\nabla)u=\\sum_{j=1}^3u_j\\partial_{x_j}u$, and $(\\Delta u)_i=\\partial_{xx}u_i+\\partial_{yy}u_i+\\partial_{zz}u_i$ for $i=1,2,3$. Suppose $u$ is an already-existing solution of the constant-density incompressible Navier-Stokes equation $u_t+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u+\\rho_0^{-1}\\nabla p-\\nu\\Delta u=0$, $\\nabla\\cdot u=0$, with wall values $u|_{y=0}=0$ and $u|_{y=h}=Sh(1,0,0)$. Define $w=u-U_b$ and $E(t)=\\int_Q|w(t,x,y,z)|^2\\,dx\\,dy\\,dz$. If $|S|h^2/\\nu<2$, then for every $t\\in[0,T]$,\n\n$$E(t)\\le \\exp\\!\\left[-\\left(\\frac{2\\nu}{h^2}-|S|\\right)t\\right]E(0).$$\n\nThe condition is sufficient and is not asserted to be sharp. The estimate holds only on the interval where the assumed smooth solution exists; it proves neither existence on all time nor global three-dimensional regularity or a physical transition threshold."