        {
          "id": "thm-fd-x11",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "title": "Linear vortex-sheet growing modes",
          "statement": "For $x,t\\in\\mathbb R$, assume the fields are independent of a third spatial coordinate. Let $H_+=[0,\\infty)$ and $H_-=(-\\infty,0]$. The real-valued interface displacement is a $C^\\infty$ map $\\eta:\\mathbb R_x\\times\\mathbb R_t\\to\\mathbb R$, and the real-valued scalar potentials are maps $\\phi_\\pm:\\mathbb R_x\\times H_\\pm\\times\\mathbb R_t\\to\\mathbb R$. Each $\\phi_\\pm$ is $C^\\infty$ on the open half-domain; $\\phi_\\pm$ and their first partial derivatives $\\partial_x\\phi_\\pm$, $\\partial_y\\phi_\\pm$, and $\\partial_t\\phi_\\pm$ admit continuous one-sided traces at $y=0$, locally in $(x,t)$. On the open half-domains use ordinary pointwise Euclidean derivatives in $(x,y)$ and set $\\Delta=\\partial_{xx}+\\partial_{yy}$. Let $U_+,U_-\\in\\mathbb R$ be constant horizontal base velocities, and let the two half-planes $y>0$ and $y<0$ have the same constant density $\\rho_0>0$. The potentials satisfy $\\Delta\\phi_\\pm=0$ and, for every fixed $(x,t)$, $\\phi_\\pm$, $\\partial_x\\phi_\\pm$, $\\partial_y\\phi_\\pm$, and $\\partial_t\\phi_\\pm$ tend to zero as $y\\to+\\infty$ and $y\\to-\\infty$ respectively. The stipulated linear interface equations, interpreted by one-sided traces at $y=0$, are $(\\partial_t+U_\\pm\\partial_x)\\eta=\\partial_y\\phi_\\pm$ and continuity of the defined pressure perturbations $p'_\\pm(x,t)=-\\rho_0[\\partial_t\\phi_\\pm+U_\\pm\\partial_x\\phi_\\pm]_{y=0^\\pm}$. Gravity and surface tension are absent. Complexify this real linear system for the following normal-mode calculation.\n\nFor every real $k>0$, every nonzero complex amplitude $H$, and every $A_+,A_-,\\lambda\\in\\mathbb C$, consider a mode of the form $\\eta=H e^{ikx+\\lambda t}$, $\\phi_+=A_+e^{-ky}e^{ikx+\\lambda t}$, $\\phi_-=A_-e^{ky}e^{ikx+\\lambda t}$. Set $\\overline U=(U_++U_-)/2$ and $\\Delta U=U_+-U_-$. The mode equations require\n\n$$\\lambda=-ik\\overline U\\pm \\frac{k|\\Delta U|}{2}.$$\n\nIf $\\Delta U\\ne0$, one root has positive real part $k|\\Delta U|/2$, and its real or imaginary part gives a real exponentially growing solution of the stated linear system.\n\nFor the $2\\pi$-periodic interface, require the fields to be periodic in $x$ and set $\\mathbb T_{2\\pi}=\\mathbb R/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)$. For a complex-valued trigonometric polynomial $\\eta$ on this torus, define $\\widehat\\eta_j=(2\\pi)^{-1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\eta(x)e^{-ijx}\\,dx$ and, for $m\\ge0$, $\\|\\eta\\|_{H^m}^2=\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}(1+j^2)^m|\\widehat\\eta_j|^2$. Let $H^m(\\mathbb T_{2\\pi};\\mathbb C)$ be the completion of the complex-valued trigonometric polynomials in this norm; real-valued fields are viewed as elements of its real subspace. If $\\Delta U\\ne0$, then for every fixed $t>0$ this exact mode family has bounded initial $H^m$ norm but unbounded evolved $H^m$ norm as its integer wavenumber $k\\to\\infty$. Thus no uniform estimate of the form $\\|\\eta(t)\\|_{H^m}\\le C_t\\|\\eta(0)\\|_{H^m}$ holds for this family. This is a linear interface-model conclusion, not a theorem of nonlinear turbulent transition, viscous shear-layer behavior, or physical instability in a particular experiment.",
