          "title": "Bounded-domain Stokes residual estimate — formal worked field",
          "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $L_x,L_z,h,\\mu>0$, define $\\mathbb T_L=\\mathbb R/(L\\mathbb Z)$ for $L>0$, and set $Q=\\mathbb T_{L_x}\\times(0,h)\\times\\mathbb T_{L_z}$. Regard fields on $Q$ as periodic lifts to $\\mathbb R\\times(0,h)\\times\\mathbb R$ with periods $L_x,L_z$ in $x,z$, and integrate with Lebesgue measure over one fundamental cell in each periodic coordinate. Set $w(x,y,z)=(\\sin(\\pi y/h),0,0)$, $q=0$, and $f=\\mu(\\pi/h)^2w$. Then $w$ is a smooth divergence-free periodic field with zero trace on both $y$ walls and satisfies $-\\mu\\Delta w+\\nabla q=f$. Its norms satisfy $\\|w\\|_{L^2(Q)}^2=L_xL_z h/2$ and $\\|\\nabla w\\|_{L^2(Q)}^2=(\\pi/h)^2\\|w\\|_{L^2(Q)}^2$, so $\\|w\\|_{L^2(Q)}=(h/\\pi)\\|\\nabla w\\|_{L^2(Q)}\\le h\\|\\nabla w\\|_{L^2(Q)}$. This is a formal worked field for the periodic-channel Poincare clause; the general bounded $C^1$ Euclidean residual theorem is not applied to this periodic cell.",
