          "kind": "example",
          "title": "Vanishing scalar density leaves velocity undetermined by momentum",
          "statement": "At a fixed point, let $\\rho\\in[0,\\infty)$ be mass density and let $u\\in\\mathbb R^3$ be velocity. In SI units, $\\rho$ is measured in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$, $u$ in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, and the momentum-density vector $M=\\rho u$ in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}$. For the field interpretation, use the restricted Lebesgue spaces, measurable-map and almost-everywhere conventions of the conservative weak-form definition under Countable Choice; the pointwise map $u\\mapsto\\rho u$ below is purely algebraic. To use momentum density as a field in a conservative weak form, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open and $T>0$, take measurable maps $\\rho:\\Omega\\times[0,T)\\to[0,\\infty)$ and $u:\\Omega\\times[0,T)\\to\\mathbb R^3$, and require their product $U(x,t):=\\rho(x,t)u(x,t)$ to be locally integrable, including near $t=0$. The vector $M$ above is a pointwise value of such a product, not itself a field. At $\\rho=0$, distinct vectors $u_1\\ne u_2$ both give $M=0$, so the momentum density does not determine velocity. At $\\rho>0$, the relation has the unique inverse $u=M/\\rho$. This is a pointwise algebraic example and makes no claim that any chosen fields solve a differential equation.",
