          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume countable choice. Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval containing $0$, let $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be a bounded open reference region with $C^1$ boundary (locally a $C^1$ graph with the region on one side), and let $U$ be an open neighborhood of $\\overline B$. Let $\\chi\\in C^2(I\\times U;\\mathbb R^3)$ have orientation-preserving diffeomorphic time slices (bijections onto open images with $C^2$ inverses and positive Jacobian determinant) and satisfy $\\chi(0,a)=a$, and set $\\Omega_t=\\chi_t(B)$ and $\\mathcal O=\\{(t,x):t\\in I,\\ x\\in\\chi_t(U)\\}$. Here $C^r$ means all coordinate partial derivatives through order $r$ are continuous. Let $\\rho:\\mathcal O\\to(0,\\infty)$, $u:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R^3$, $e:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R$, $q:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R^3$, and $\\sigma:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}$ be $C^1$ on $\\mathcal O$. Let $b:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and $r:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. Require $u(t,\\chi(t,a))=\\partial_t\\chi(t,a)$ for every $(t,a)\\in I\\times U$, so $u$ is the velocity induced by this same material motion. Their SI units are respectively kilograms per cubic metre, metres per second, joules per kilogram, watts per square metre, pascals, metres per second squared, and watts per kilogram. Put $k=|u|^2/2$, $E=e+k$, $D_xu=(\\partial_{x_j}u_i)_{ij}$, $D=(D_xu+(D_xu)^{\\mathsf T})/2$, $D_t=\\partial_t+u\\cdot\\nabla_x$, and $A:B=\\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}B_{ij}$. Use rowwise divergence $(\\operatorname{div}\\sigma)_i=\\sum_j\\partial_{x_j}\\sigma_{ij}$, vector divergence $\\operatorname{div}q=\\sum_j\\partial_{x_j}q_j$, and traction $t=\\sigma n$, where $n$ is the outward Euclidean unit normal and $q\\cdot n>0$ denotes heat leaving the material region.\n\nAssume pointwise mass continuity and, on every material region $P_t=\\chi_t(V)$ where $V$ is a nonempty bounded open reference region with compact closure in $B$ and $C^1$ boundary locally a graph with the region on one side, the adopted linear-momentum, angular-momentum, and total-energy balances\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{P_t}\\rho u\\,dx=\\int_{P_t}\\rho b\\,dx+\\int_{\\partial P_t}\\sigma n\\,dS,$$\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{P_t}x\\times(\\rho u)\\,dx=\\int_{P_t}x\\times(\\rho b)\\,dx+\\int_{\\partial P_t}x\\times(\\sigma n)\\,dS,$$\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{P_t}\\rho E\\,dx=\\int_{P_t}(\\rho b\\cdot u+\\rho r)\\,dx+\\int_{\\partial P_t}\\bigl(u\\cdot\\sigma n-q\\cdot n\\bigr)\\,dS.$$\nThe angular balance is about a fixed inertial origin and there are no surface couples or intrinsic spin. Then throughout the balanced physical region $\\mathcal O_B:=\\{(t,x):t\\in I,\\ x\\in\\chi_t(B)\\}$, with $\\mathcal O$ retained only as the ambient regularity domain,\n$$\\partial_t(\\rho u)+\\operatorname{div}_x(\\rho u\\otimes u-\\sigma)=\\rho b,$$\n$$\\sigma=\\sigma^{\\mathsf T},$$\n$$\\partial_t(\\rho E)+\\operatorname{div}_x(\\rho E u-\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}u+q)=\\rho b\\cdot u+\\rho r,$$\nand\n$$\\partial_t(\\rho k)+\\operatorname{div}_x(\\rho k u-\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}u)=\\rho b\\cdot u-\\sigma:D,\\qquad \\rho D_t e=\\sigma:D-\\operatorname{div}_xq+\\rho r.$$\nConversely, the pointwise momentum equation and symmetric stress imply the angular-momentum balance, and the pointwise total-energy equation implies its stated material integral balance. All deductions are conditional on the smooth model and do not assert existence of a solution.",
