          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice: every sequence of nonempty sets admits a choice function. Use the restricted complete Euclidean Lebesgue spaces, almost-everywhere identification and signed integrals of the conservative weak-form definition. Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be a nonempty open spatial region and $I=(0,T_f)$ a time interval in seconds, with $T_f>0$. Let $\\Sigma$ be a $C^1$ moving interface in $\\Omega\\times I$ that locally is the graph $x_3=h(t,x_1,x_2)$ and separates minus and plus sides; here $C^1$ means that the graph function and its first coordinate partial derivatives are continuous. Let $n$ be the dimensionless Euclidean unit normal pointing from minus to plus, and let $V_n$ be the signed normal speed of $\\Sigma$ in metres per second. For a field with one-sided traces, define $[g]:=g_+-g_-$. Let $\\rho\\ge0$ be mass density in kilograms per cubic metre, $u$ velocity in metres per second, $\\sigma$ Cauchy stress in pascals, $e$ specific internal energy in joules per kilogram, $q$ outward-positive heat flux in watts per square metre, $T>0$ absolute temperature in kelvins, $s$ specific entropy in joules per kilogram kelvin, $b$ body acceleration in metres per second squared, and $r$ specific heat supply in watts per kilogram. Set $E:=e+|u|^2/2$ in joules per kilogram. Assume these fields are $C^1$ on each side and, for every relatively compact interface patch, admit $C^1$ one-sided extensions to its closed sides; here $C^1$ means that the fields and all their first time and spatial coordinate partial derivatives are continuous on those extensions. In particular, the resulting one-sided traces are finite and continuous. Assume that all displayed products and sources are locally integrable. The sources are ordinary volume functions and have no measure concentrated on $\\Sigma$. Use traction $t=\\sigma n$, $(u\\otimes u)_{ij}=u_i u_j$, and rowwise matrix divergence $(\\operatorname{div}F)_i=\\sum_j\\partial_{x_j}F_{ij}$.\n\nOn each side suppose the classical equations hold:\n\n$$\\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}(\\rho u)=0,$$\n\n$$\\partial_t(\\rho u)+\\operatorname{div}(\\rho u\\otimes u-\\sigma)=\\rho b,$$\n\n$$\\partial_t(\\rho E)+\\operatorname{div}(\\rho E u-\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}u+q)=\\rho b\\cdot u+\\rho r,$$\n\n$$\\partial_t(\\rho s)+\\operatorname{div}(\\rho s u+q/T)\\ge\\rho r/T.$$\n\nAdopt the separate weak mass, momentum and total-energy identities and physical entropy inequality. Their interior restrictions use compactly supported $C^1$ tests in $\\Omega\\times I$, away from $t=0$ and $t=T_f$. The full weak forms may also use tests nonzero at $t=0$ and include initial-data terms; those terms are separate data and are not determined by the interface jumps. The interior weak conservation equations across $\\Sigma$ hold if and only if the side equations hold and\n\n$$[\\rho u]\\cdot n=V_n[\\rho],$$\n\n$$[\\rho u\\otimes u-\\sigma]n=V_n[\\rho u],$$\n\n$$[\\rho E u-\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}u+q]\\cdot n=V_n[\\rho E].$$\n\nFor the entropy jump characterization, assume additionally that the vector field $q/T$ admits $C^1$ one-sided extensions to every relatively compact interface patch, with the same meaning of extension as above. This condition holds in particular if the temperature traces $T_\\pm$ are strictly positive. It does not require positive temperature traces when the quotient itself extends; in that case $(q/T)_\\pm$ denotes the trace of the quotient, not division of the separate traces. No extension or boundedness of the locally integrable source $\\rho r/T$ is assumed. Under this additional flux hypothesis, the interior weak physical entropy inequality holds across the interface if and only if it holds on both sides and\n\n$$[\\rho s u+q/T]\\cdot n-V_n[\\rho s]\\ge0.$$\n\nEquivalently, for any conservative density $U$ and flux $F$ above, the interface coefficient is $[F]n-V_n[U]$. Here $[F]n$ is matrix–vector multiplication for momentum; for a scalar law its vector flux is identified with a one-row matrix, so $[F]n=[F]\\cdot n$. The coefficient must vanish for conservation and be nonnegative for physical entropy. These conditions apply only to the specified piecewise $C^1$ fields and traces. They neither establish existence or uniqueness of a multidimensional shock solution nor extend smooth material equations through arbitrary nonsmooth fields.",
