          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval containing $0$, let $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be a nonempty bounded open reference region with $C^1$ boundary (locally a $C^1$ graph with the region on one side), and let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be an open neighborhood of $\\overline B$. Let $\\chi\\in C^2(I\\times U;\\mathbb R^3)$ have orientation-preserving diffeomorphic time slices (bijections onto open images with $C^2$ inverses and positive Jacobian determinant) and satisfy $\\chi(0,a)=a$. Here $C^r$ means all coordinate partial derivatives through order $r$ are continuous. Set $\\Omega_t=\\chi_t(B)$ and $\\mathcal O=\\{(t,x):t\\in I,\\ x\\in\\chi_t(U)\\}$. Use SI units in a classical Cartesian inertial frame with absolute time; inertial changes have form $t^*=t+t_0$, $x^*=Qx+ct+d$, where $SO(3)=\\{Q\\in\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}:Q^{\\mathsf T}Q=I_3,\\det Q=1\\}$, $c$ is a constant relative velocity in metres per second, and $d$ is a constant spatial translation in metres. Let $\\rho_0\\in C^1(\\overline B)$ and $\\rho\\in C^1(\\mathcal O)$ be positive mass densities in kilograms per cubic metre, let $u\\in C^1(\\mathcal O;\\mathbb R^3)$ be the velocity induced by this motion, so $u(t,\\chi(t,a))=\\partial_t\\chi(t,a)$ for every $(t,a)\\in I\\times U$, measured in metres per second, and let $b\\in C^0(\\mathcal O;\\mathbb R^3)$ be body acceleration in metres per second squared. Let $\\sigma\\in C^1(\\mathcal O;\\mathbb R^{3\\times3})$ be Cauchy stress in pascals, with traction $t(t,x,n)=\\sigma(t,x)n$ for every outward Euclidean unit normal $n$. For every nonempty bounded open reference region $V$ whose closure is compact in $B$ and whose $C^1$ boundary is locally a graph with the region on one side, put $P_t=\\chi_t(V)$. Adopt on each $P_t$ the material mass, linear-momentum and angular-momentum balances:\n\n$$\\int_{P_t}\\rho(t,x)\\,dx=\\int_V\\rho_0(a)\\,da,\\qquad \\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{P_t}\\rho u\\,dx=\\int_{P_t}\\rho b\\,dx+\\int_{\\partial P_t}t(t,x,n)\\,dS,$$\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{P_t}x\\times\\rho u\\,dx=\\int_{P_t}x\\times\\rho b\\,dx+\\int_{\\partial P_t}x\\times t(t,x,n)\\,dS.$$\n\nHere $dS$ is Euclidean surface measure, with chart density $\\sqrt{\\det(DY^{\\mathsf T}DY)}$. The angular momentum equation is about a fixed inertial origin; the model has no surface couples or intrinsic spin.\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. The adopted traction representation is pointwise unique. The local momentum and stress-symmetry conclusions below hold on the balanced physical region $\\mathcal O_B:=\\{(t,x):t\\in I,\\ x\\in\\Omega_t\\}$; the larger $\\mathcal O$ supplies ambient regularity only. With $D_tu:=\\partial_tu+(D_xu)u$ and rowwise divergence $(\\operatorname{div}\\sigma)_i:=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\partial_{x_j}\\sigma_{ij}$, the local momentum equation is\n\n$$\\rho D_tu=\\rho b+\\operatorname{div}\\sigma,$$\n\nequivalently\n\n$$\\partial_t(\\rho u)+\\operatorname{div}(\\rho u\\otimes u-\\sigma)=\\rho b,$$\n\nwhere $(u\\otimes u)_{ij}=u_i u_j$. The angular momentum balance with no couples implies $\\sigma=\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}$. Conversely, in this same smooth positive-density setting, the local momentum equation with symmetric $\\sigma$ implies the angular momentum balance. The model postulate adopts the traction representation; this theorem establishes its local balance consequences and uniqueness.",
