          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval containing $0$, let $B\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be a nonempty bounded open reference region with $C^1$ boundary (locally a $C^1$ graph with the region on one side), and let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be an open neighborhood of $\\overline B$. Let $\\chi\\in C^2(I\\times U;\\mathbb R^3)$ be a motion with orientation-preserving diffeomorphic time slices (bijections onto open images with $C^2$ inverses and positive Jacobian determinant) and $\\chi(0,a)=a$. Here $C^r$ means all coordinate partial derivatives through order $r$ are continuous. Set $\\Omega_t=\\chi_t(B)$ and $\\mathcal O=\\{(t,x):t\\in I,\\ x\\in\\chi_t(U)\\}$. Use SI units in a classical Cartesian inertial frame. Let $\\rho_0\\in C^1(\\overline B)$ and $\\rho\\in C^1(\\mathcal O;(0,\\infty))$ be positive densities, and let $u:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R^3$, $e:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R$, $q:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R^3$, and $\\sigma:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R^{3\\times3}$ be $C^1$, with $u(t,\\chi(t,a))=\\partial_t\\chi(t,a)$ for every $(t,a)\\in I\\times U$; let $b:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R^3$ and $r:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R$ be continuous. Their units are respectively kilograms per cubic metre, metres per second, joules per kilogram, watts per square metre, pascals, metres per second squared, and watts per kilogram. Define $k:=|u|^2/2$, $E:=e+k$, $D_xu=(\\partial_{x_j}u_i)_{ij}$, $D:=(D_xu+(D_xu)^{\\mathsf T})/2$, $D_t:=\\partial_t+u\\cdot\\nabla_x$, and $A:B:=\\sum_{i,j}A_{ij}B_{ij}$. Use rowwise divergence $(\\operatorname{div}\\sigma)_i=\\sum_j\\partial_{x_j}\\sigma_{ij}$ and vector divergence $\\operatorname{div}q=\\sum_j\\partial_{x_j}q_j$. For every nonempty bounded open $V$ whose closure is compact in $B$ and whose $C^1$ boundary is locally a graph with the region on one side, put $P_t=\\chi_t(V)$ and let $n$ be its outward Euclidean unit normal. Use Euclidean surface measure $dS$ with chart density $\\sqrt{\\det(DY^{\\mathsf T}DY)}$.\n\nAdopt, on each such material region,\n\n$$\\int_{P_t}\\rho\\,dx=\\int_V\\rho_0\\,da,\\qquad \\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{P_t}\\rho u\\,dx=\\int_{P_t}\\rho b\\,dx+\\int_{\\partial P_t}\\sigma n\\,dS,$$\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{P_t}x\\times\\rho u\\,dx=\\int_{P_t}x\\times\\rho b\\,dx+\\int_{\\partial P_t}x\\times\\sigma n\\,dS,$$\n\n$$\\frac{d}{dt}\\int_{P_t}\\rho E\\,dx=\\int_{P_t}(\\rho b\\cdot u+\\rho r)\\,dx+\\int_{\\partial P_t}\\bigl(u\\cdot\\sigma n-q\\cdot n\\bigr)\\,dS.$$\n\nThe angular-momentum balance is about a fixed inertial origin; assume no surface couples or intrinsic spin. Assume also the Axiom of Choice. On the balanced physical region $\\mathcal O_B:=\\{(t,x):t\\in I,\\ x\\in\\Omega_t\\}$, angular momentum gives $\\sigma=\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}$, and the local total-energy balance is\n\n$$\\partial_t(\\rho E)+\\operatorname{div}(\\rho E u-\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}u+q)=\\rho b\\cdot u+\\rho r.$$\n\nIt splits into the kinetic and internal-energy balances\n\n$$\\partial_t(\\rho k)+\\operatorname{div}(\\rho k u-\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}u)=\\rho b\\cdot u-\\sigma:D,$$\n\n$$\\rho D_t e=\\sigma:D-\\operatorname{div}q+\\rho r.$$\n\nThus the boundary power flux is $-\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}u$ and the outward heat flux is $q$. If additionally $b=-\\nabla_x\\Phi$ for a time-independent $C^1$ scalar potential $\\Phi$ defined on an open spatial set containing every $\\Omega_t$, measured in joules per kilogram, and $r=0$, then\n\n$$\\partial_t\\bigl(\\rho(E+\\Phi)\\bigr)+\\operatorname{div}\\bigl(\\rho(E+\\Phi)u-\\sigma^{\\mathsf T}u+q\\bigr)=0.$$\n\nConsequently $\\int_C\\rho(E+\\Phi)\\,dx$ is constant on any bounded fixed $C^1$ region $C$ with closure contained in the fluid region throughout $I$, with no-slip $u=0$ and insulation $q\\cdot n=0$, or on a periodic cell $Q=\\prod_{j=1}^3[0,L_j]$ with side lengths $L_j>0$ metres, $Q\\subset\\Omega_t$ for every $t\\in I$, and opposite faces identified by translations when all fields and $\\Phi$ are periodic. All local equations above 