          "statement": "Let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be an open time interval, let $\\Omega\\subset I\\times\\mathbb R^3$ be open, and let $P:(0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb R$ be a continuously differentiable pressure function. A smooth positive-density barotropic Euler solution is a pair of fields $\\rho\\in C^2(\\Omega,(0,\\infty))$ and $u\\in C^2(\\Omega,\\mathbb R^3)$ satisfying\n$$ \\partial_t\\rho+\\operatorname{div}(\\rho u)=0,\\qquad \\rho\\bigl(\\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u\\bigr)+\\nabla P(\\rho)=0. $$\nHere $\\operatorname{div}u=\\sum_{i=1}^3\\partial_{x_i}u_i$, $\\nabla$ is the spatial gradient, and $D_tu=\\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u$ is the material acceleration. The constitutive stress is $\\sigma=-P(\\rho)I_3$ under the tension-positive traction convention. Thus “barotropic” means that pressure is a specified function of density alone, and “Euler” here means that the viscous stress is absent. No sign of $P'(\\rho)$ is built into the definition; a positive acoustic response is a separate hypothesis where used. In SI interpretation, $\\rho$ is in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$, $u$ in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, $P$ in $\\mathrm{Pa}$, and $P'(\\rho)$ in $\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-2}}$. The model gives equations for a smooth solution and asserts no existence theorem.",
