          "statement": "Let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be an open time interval. Use the Cartesian inertial-chart convention of the adopted continuum balances on Newtonian spacetime $I\\times E$: $E$ is an oriented Euclidean affine three-space, and the event-coordinate map is $(t,X)\\mapsto(t,x_t(X))$ with $x_t(X)=b(X)+tw$, where $b:E\\to\\mathbb R^3$ is an orientation-preserving affine isometry and $w\\in\\mathbb R^3$ is constant. Time is absolute and measured in seconds, spatial coordinates in metres, and $w$ in metres per second. All derivatives below use this fixed chart. Let $\\Omega\\subset I\\times\\mathbb R^3$ be open. A smooth constant-density incompressible Newtonian branch has a constant $\\rho_0>0$ in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$, a constant dynamic shear viscosity $\\mu\\ge0$ in $\\mathrm{Pa\\,s}$, a velocity field $u\\in C^2(\\Omega,\\mathbb R^3)$ measured in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, and a pressure multiplier $p\\in C^1(\\Omega,\\mathbb R)$ measured in $\\mathrm{Pa}$. Define $L_{ij}=\\partial_{x_j}u_i$, $D=(L+L^T)/2$, and $D_tu=\\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u$. The branch is specified by\n$$ \\rho=\\rho_0,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}u=0,\\qquad \\sigma=-pI_3+2\\mu D,\\qquad \\rho_0D_tu=\\operatorname{div}\\sigma, $$\nwhere $(\\operatorname{div}\\sigma)_i=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\partial_{x_j}\\sigma_{ij}$. Equivalently, for constant $\\mu$, its momentum equation is\n$$ \\rho_0D_tu+\\nabla p=\\mu\\Delta u,\\qquad \\Delta u_i=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\partial_{x_jx_j}u_i. $$\nIndeed, $\\operatorname{div}(2\\mu D)=\\mu\\Delta u+\\mu\\nabla(\\operatorname{div}u)=\\mu\\Delta u$. This is the incompressible specialization of the adopted Newtonian closure: $\\theta=\\operatorname{div}u=0$ implies $D_0=D$, so the bulk-viscosity and Fourier-flux terms do not enter this mechanical equation. The pressure is a constraint multiplier and is not identified with a compressible equation-of-state pressure. Define the kinematic viscosity $\\nu=\\mu/\\rho_0$, in $\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-1}}$. Boundary and initial data are not part of this definition, and the item asserts no solution existence.",
