          "empirical_premises": [],
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open time interval and $\\Omega\\subset I\\times\\mathbb R^3$ a nonempty open domain for the model fields. Let $e\\in C^2(\\mathcal U)$ be the local specific-energy relation on an open $\\mathcal U\\subset(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R$, with state coordinates $(v,s)$, $T=e_s$, and $p=-e_v$; each state partial derivative holds the other coordinate fixed. Fix $(v_b,s_b)\\in\\mathcal U$ with $T_b>0$ and $e_{ss}(v_b,s_b)>0$, set $\\rho_b=1/v_b$, and take a barotropic pressure law $P\\in C^1((0,\\infty))$ that agrees with $p(1/\\rho,s_b)$ on an open density interval $J_\\rho\\subset(0,\\infty)$ containing $\\rho_b$, with $(1/\\rho,s_b)\\in\\mathcal U$ for every $\\rho\\in J_\\rho$. This is the fixed-entropy restriction of the adopted local state relation. The smooth barotropic Euler model is linearized about the uniform rest state $(\\rho,u)=(\\rho_b,0)$. For $C^2$ perturbation fields $\\delta\\rho:I\\times\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R$ and $w:I\\times\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$, the first variation at $\\varepsilon=0$ after $\\rho=\\rho_b+\\varepsilon\\delta\\rho$, $u=\\varepsilon w$ is\n$$ \\partial_t\\delta\\rho+\\rho_b\\operatorname{div}w=0,\\qquad \\rho_b\\partial_tw+P'(\\rho_b)\\nabla\\delta\\rho=0,\\qquad \\partial_{tt}\\delta\\rho=c_s^2\\Delta\\delta\\rho, \\quad c_s^2=P'(\\rho_b)=v_b^2e_{vv}(v_b,s_b). $$\nThe sign of $c_s^2$ is not assumed in the base model. If $c_s^2>0$, define $c_s=\\sqrt{c_s^2}$. For every nonzero $k\\in\\mathbb R^3$, with $\\omega=c_s|k|$, the fields\n$$ \\delta\\rho=A\\cos(k\\cdot x-\\omega t),\\qquad w=\\frac{\\omega A}{\\rho_b|k|^2}k\\cos(k\\cdot x-\\omega t) $$\nsolve the first-variation system for every real amplitude $A$. They are exact solutions of the linearized equations, not finite-amplitude approximations with an automatic error or lifespan bound. No empirical value of $c_s$ is asserted.\n\nIn SI units, $t$ is in seconds, $x$ in metres, $\\rho_b,\\delta\\rho,A$ in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$, $u,w,c_s$ in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, $k$ in $\\mathrm{m^{-1}}$, $\\omega$ in $\\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, and $c_s^2$ in $\\mathrm{m^2\\,s^{-2}}$.",
