          "physical_scope": "Conditional smooth local-equilibrium calculation for an explicitly adopted calorically perfect gas with positive specific gas constant and specific heat. The temperature equation includes stress, heat flux and heat supply; entropy constancy and the isentropic pressure law are restricted to the stated adiabatic, inviscid, unsourced branch. No empirical gas data or universal gas model is asserted.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the complete smooth material, state, contact and mass/momentum/angular-momentum/total-energy balance hypotheses of the mathematical Gibbs-entropy identity, including $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, as stated in Facts & Assumptions. Let $R>0$ be a specific gas constant and $c_v>0$ a constant specific heat at constant volume, both in $\\mathrm{J\\,kg^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. Choose reference values $T_{\\mathrm{ref}}>0$ in $\\mathrm K$, $\\rho_{\\mathrm{ref}}>0$ in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$, and $s_{\\mathrm{ref}}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,kg^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. On $v>0,\\ s\\in\\mathbb R$, adopt\n$$ T(v,s)=T_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{s-s_{\\mathrm{ref}}}{c_v}\\right) \\left(\\frac{1}{\\rho_{\\mathrm{ref}}v}\\right)^{R/c_v},\\qquad e(v,s)=c_vT(v,s),\\qquad p(v,s)=\\frac{R\\,T(v,s)}{v}. $$\nThese formulas give $T=e_s>0$, $p=-e_v$, and $e\\in C^2((0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R)$; equivalently, with $\\rho=1/v$, $s=s_{\\mathrm{ref}}+c_v\\ln(T/T_{\\mathrm{ref}})-R\\ln(\\rho/\\rho_{\\mathrm{ref}})$ and $p=\\rho RT$. The logarithms have dimensionless arguments.\n\nFor a smooth solution, define $\\theta=\\operatorname{div}u$, $\\sigma=-pI_3+\\tau$, and the local entropy residual $\\mathcal G=\\rho D_ts+\\operatorname{div}(q/T)-\\rho r/T$. The temperature balance is\n$$ \\rho c_vD_tT=-p\\theta+\\tau:D-\\operatorname{div}q+\\rho r. $$\nOn the additional adiabatic, inviscid, unsourced branch $\\tau=0,\\ q=0,\\ r=0$, one has $D_ts=0$. Along any trajectory on which the entropy value is $s_0$, the pressure law is\n$$ p=K(s_0)\\rho^\\gamma,\\qquad \\gamma=1+\\frac{R}{c_v},\\qquad K(s_0)=RT_{\\mathrm{ref}}\\exp\\!\\left(\\frac{s_0-s_{\\mathrm{ref}}}{c_v}\\right) \\rho_{\\mathrm{ref}}^{-R/c_v}>0. $$\nHere $\\gamma$ is dimensionless and $K(s_0)$ has SI units $\\mathrm{Pa}\\,(\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}})^{-\\gamma}$, so $K(s_0)\\rho^\\gamma$ has units of pressure. The factor $K(s_0)$ can differ between trajectories with different entropy values. The gas closure is an adopted model