          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "physical_scope": "Given selected stated law and balances, derive objective nonnegative mechanical dissipation; conformation entropy requires explicit isothermal Helmholtz realization/thermostat.",
          "statement": "Work in the smooth incompressible branch of the adopted nonrelativistic continuum model. Let $T>0$, let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval containing $[0,T]$, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^3$ be open, and let $u\\in C^2(J\\times\\Omega;\\mathbb R^3)$ be the Eulerian velocity induced by a $C^2$ material motion with orientation-preserving $C^2$ diffeomorphic time slices, and satisfy $\\operatorname{div}u=0$. At a spatial point let $L=\\nabla u$, $D=(L+L^{\\mathsf T})/2$, $\\operatorname{tr}D=0$, and use the Frobenius contraction $S:T=\\operatorname{tr}(S^{\\mathsf T}T)$ with norm $|S|=(S:S)^{1/2}$. Let $M_3(\\mathbb R)$ denote the real $3\\times3$ matrices, and define $\\mathrm{Sym}_0(3)=\\{S\\in M_3(\\mathbb R):S=S^{\\mathsf T},\\operatorname{tr}S=0\\}$ and $\\mathrm{Sym}^+(3)=\\{C\\in M_3(\\mathbb R):C=C^{\\mathsf T},z^{\\mathsf T}Cz>0\\text{ for every }z\\ne0\\}$. A constitutive response is objective when under every rigid observer change $x^*=Q(t)x+c(t)$, with $Q\\in C^2([0,T],\\mathrm{SO}(3))$ and $c\\in C^2([0,T],\\mathbb R^3)$, its second-order tensor value at corresponding events transforms as $S^*=QSQ^{\\mathsf T}$. For the generalized Newtonian branch, let $\\dot\\gamma=(2D:D)^{1/2}$, let $\\eta:[0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)$ be finite and continuous, and set $\\tau=2\\eta(\\dot\\gamma)D$. Define $I_3$ as the $3\\times3$ identity matrix; in SI units, $L,D,\\dot\\gamma$ have units $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, $\\eta,\\eta_s,\\mu_p$ have units $\\mathrm{Pa\\,s}$, and $\\tau,\\tau_y,G$ have units Pa. For the Bingham branch, let $\\mu_p\\ge0$ in $\\mathrm{Pa\\,s}$ and $\\tau_y\\ge0$ in $\\mathrm{Pa}$, and set $\\mathcal B(D)=\\{2\\mu_pD+\\tau_yD/|D|\\}$ for $D\\ne0$ and $\\mathcal B(0)=\\{S\\in\\mathrm{Sym}_0(3):|S|\\le\\tau_y\\}$. For the conformation branch, take a $C^1$ trajectory $x:[0,T]\\to\\Omega$ with $x'(t)=u(t,x(t))$. Set $L(t)=\\nabla u(t,x(t))$, $D(t)=(L(t)+L(t)^{\\mathsf T})/2$, and let the dimensionless $C\\in C^1([0,T];M_3(\\mathbb R))$ be symmetric, so $C(t)=C(t)^{\\mathsf T}$ for all $t$, and satisfy\n\n$$\nC'=LC+CL^{\\mathsf T}-(C-I_3)/\\lambda,\\qquad C(0)=C_0\\in\\mathrm{Sym}^+(3),\n$$\n\nwhere $G>0$ is a modulus in Pa, $\\lambda>0$ is a relaxation time in seconds, and $\\eta_s\\ge0$ is a solvent viscosity in Pa s. Define $\\tau_p=G(C-I_3)$, $\\tau_s=2\\eta_sD$, and $w(C)=\\frac G2(\\operatorname{tr}C-\\log\\det C-3)$ in J m$^{-3}$. Then the generalized Newtonian and Bingham branches have objective stress responses and nonnegative instantaneous mechanical power $\\tau:D\\ge0$ for every allowed stress. On every forward time $t\\in[0,T]$, the conformation solution remains positive definite, its constitutive response is objective, and\n\n$$\n(\\tau_s+\\tau_p):D-\\frac{d}{dt}w(C) =2\\eta_sD:D+\\frac{G}{2\\lambda}\\bigl(\\operatorname{tr}C+\\operatorname{tr}C^{-1}-6\\bigr)\\ge0.\n$$\n\nThus the selected smooth branches have nonnegative mechanical or stored-energy dissipation under their stated model hypotheses. This is a conditional deduction, not a claim of empirical material applicability or existence of a fluid partial-differential-equation solution.\n\nFor an entropy-production interpretation of the conformation inequality, additionally adopt the following separate isothermal realization along the trajectory. Fix $T_0>0$ in K and let $e_v,s_v\\in C^1([0,T];\\mathbb R)$ be internal-energy and entropy densities per current volume, in $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$ and $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}m^{-3}}$, respectively. Let $h\\in C^0([0,T];\\mathbb R)$ be the signed heat-removal rate per current volume, in $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-3}}$, positive for heat passed to an ideal external thermostat at the same temperature $T_0$. Assume this is the only heat exchange, carrying entropy out at rate $h/T_0$, and adopt the energy balance $e_v'=(\\tau_s+\\tau_p):D-h$ and the Helmholtz free-energy identification $w(C)=e_v-T_0s_v$. Define the internal entropy-production density by $\\pi_s:=s_v'+h/T_0$, in $\\mathrm{W\\,K^{-1}m^{-3}}$. Derivatives at the time endpoints are one-sided. Then\n\n$$\nT_0\\pi_s=(\\tau_s+\\tau_p):D-\\frac{d}{dt}w(C)\\ge0.\n$$\n\nThese additional thermodynamic assumptions are not inferred from the continuum balances or the stored-energy identity alone.",
