          "title": "Full wall Stokes pressure requires a divergence inverse",
          "statement": "Assume full AC. Let $d\\ge2$, $R>0$, and let $\\Omega\\subset(-R,R)^d$ be a nonempty bounded open set. For each $\\mu>0$ and $f\\in L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^d)$, the projected stationary Stokes theorem gives a unique $u$ in the distributionally divergence-free subspace $V$ of the closure-based $H_0^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^d)$ space, satisfying\n$$\\mu\\int_\\Omega\\nabla u:\\nabla v\\,dx=\\int_\\Omega f\\cdot v\\,dx\\qquad(v\\in V),$$\nwith $\\|\\nabla u\\|_2\\le2R\\|f\\|_2/\\mu$; this conclusion contains no pressure. If $\\Omega=\\prod_{i=1}^d(a_i,b_i)\\subset(-R,R)^d$ is a nonempty finite open rectangle, the separate rectangular theorem additionally gives $p\\in H^{-1}(\\Omega)$ such that $-\\mu\\Delta u+\\nabla p=f$ as vector distributions, with $p$ unique modulo constant distributions. The rectangular result does not assert an $L^2$ pressure estimate or smoothness at rectangle corners. Neither theorem claims pressure recovery for arbitrary bounded open sets; such a claim needs a domain-appropriate divergence right inverse or inf-sup argument.",
