          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-fd-periodic-fourier-sobolev-spaces": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $d\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, $\\kappa>0$, $\\gamma>1$, and put $a=(\\gamma-1)/2$. For positive density $\\rho>0$, define the polytropic pressure $p(\\rho)=\\kappa\\rho^\\gamma$, sound speed $c(\\rho)=\\sqrt{p'(\\rho)}=\\sqrt{\\kappa\\gamma}\\,\\rho^a$, and sound-speed variable $r=c/a$. The map $\\rho\\mapsto r$ is a smooth bijection $(0,\\infty)\\to(0,\\infty)$ with inverse $\\rho=(ar/\\sqrt{\\kappa\\gamma})^{1/a}$. For a velocity $u\\in\\mathbb R^d$, set $q=(r,u_1,\\ldots,u_d)\\in\\mathbb R^{d+1}$. Let $e_0,e_1,\\ldots,e_d$ be the standard basis of $\\mathbb R^{d+1}$, with $e_0$ the scalar coordinate and $e_j$ the velocity coordinate $u_j$. Define the real matrix\n$$A_j(q)=u_j I_{d+1}+ar(e_0e_j^{\\mathsf T}+e_je_0^{\\mathsf T}),\\qquad 1\\le j\\le d.$$\nEach $A_j(q)$ is symmetric and linear in $q$; the same formula defines the linear map $A_j:\\mathbb R^{d+1}\\to\\mathbb R^{(d+1)\\times(d+1)}$ even when the scalar coordinate is nonpositive, as needed for polynomial approximations. To interpret derivatives, let $I\\subset\\mathbb R$ be an open time interval and let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be open, or let $\\Omega=Q_d=(\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^d$. Take fields $\\rho\\in C^1(I\\times\\Omega;(0,\\infty))$ and $u\\in C^1(I\\times\\Omega;\\mathbb R^d)$, and define $r(t,x)=\\sqrt{\\kappa\\gamma}\\rho(t,x)^a/a$ and $q(t,x)=(r(t,x),u(t,x))$ pointwise. Spatial derivatives on $Q_d$ are derivatives of the periodic lifts. On a closed time interval the same convention means the fields and first derivatives extend continuously to its endpoints, with one-sided time derivatives there. The first-order system $q_t+\\sum_{j=1}^d A_j(q)\\partial_jq=0$ is a pointwise equality on the time-space domain and means the pair\n$$r_t+u\\cdot\\nabla r+ar\\,\\operatorname{div}u=0,\\qquad u_t+u\\cdot\\nabla u+ar\\nabla r=0.$$\nFor periodic coefficients, let $q:Q_d\\to\\mathbb R^{d+1}$ be componentwise integrable (in particular, any continuous periodic spatial field or time slice above), and take normalized measure $d\\mu=(2\\pi)^{-d}dx$, Fourier coefficients $\\widehat q(k)=\\int q(x)e^{-ik\\cdot x}\\,d\\mu(x)$, weights $w_k=(1+|k|^2)^{1/2}$, and $\\|q\\|_{H^m}^2=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^d}w_k^{2m}|\\widehat q(k)|^2$, componentwise; real fields satisfy $\\widehat q(-k)=\\overline{\\widehat q(k)}$. The coefficient integrals are defined on these integrable fields. For general $H^m$ elements, coefficient coordinates mean those of the weighted completion in the Fourier-space definition; no integral formula is imposed on an element without an integrable representative. These are the periodic Fourier-space conventions used for $H^m$ in this development.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "literature-derived",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "François Golse, The Boltzmann Equation and Its Hydrodynamic Limits, §7.4",
                "url": "https://www.cmls.polytechnique.fr/perso/golse/Surveys/FGHdBookEvEq.pdf"
              }
            ]
          },
