        {
          "id": "thm-fd-compatible-wall-shear-heat",
          "kind": "theorem",
          "domain": "mathematics",
          "title": "Global compatible no-slip shear evolution",
          "reference": "items/thm-fd-compatible-wall-shear-heat.md",
          "design_state": "Step 1 complete draft; conditional interfaces and source scope are recorded in batch-18 notes.",
          "dependency_level": 1,
          "deps": [
            "def-fd-flat-channel-domains",
            "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus",
            "def-countable-choice"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-fd-flat-channel-domains": "mathematical-premise",
            "thm-fourier-basis-and-parseval-on-the-n-torus": "mathematical-premise",
            "def-countable-choice": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $L_1,L_2,h,\\nu>0$ and let $\\Omega=(\\mathbb R/L_1\\mathbb Z)\\times(0,h)\\times(\\mathbb R/L_2\\mathbb Z)$ with periodic coordinates $x,z$ and no-slip walls $y=0,h$. Use product Lebesgue measure $dx\\,dy\\,dz$, with the quotient coordinates carrying their period-length measures. Suppose $F:[0,h]\\to\\mathbb R$ is smooth and has a smooth odd $2h$-periodic extension to the real line. For $n\\ge1$, define $b_n=(2/h)\\int_0^hF(y)\\sin(n\\pi y/h)\\,dy$ and\n$$V(t,y)=\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty b_n e^{-\\nu(n\\pi/h)^2t}\\sin(n\\pi y/h),\\qquad t\\ge0.$$\nThen $u(t,x,y,z)=(V(t,y),0,0)$ is a global smooth real solution, unique among all smooth velocity solutions with the same initial data, of the incompressible periodic-channel system $u_t+u\\cdot\\nabla u+\\nabla\\pi=\\nu\\Delta u$, $\\operatorname{div}u=0$, $u|_{y=0,h}=0$, $u(0)=(F(y),0,0)$, with mean-zero pressure $\\pi=0$. The same sine series defines a smooth $2h$-periodic function of $y$ on the full real line. For every $t\\ge0$, $\\|u(t)\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\\le e^{-\\nu(\\pi/h)^2t}\\|u(0)\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}$. This theorem concerns the shear solution and smooth uniqueness for the same full-flow data; it gives no global regularity theorem for perturbed three-dimensional flows.",
