          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $d\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, let $s\\ge4$ be an integer, let $\\nu\\ge0$, and let $u_0:\\mathbb T^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ be smooth, real, and divergence-free, where $\\mathbb T^d=(\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^d$ has normalized Lebesgue measure and divergence-free means $\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_j(u_0)_j=0$. For a smooth periodic $f$, set $\\widehat f(k)=(2\\pi)^{-d}\\int_{[0,2\\pi]^d}f(x)e^{-ik\\cdot x}\\,dx$, $w_k=(1+|k|^2)^{1/2}$, and $\\|f\\|_{H^m}^2=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^d}w_k^{2m}|\\widehat f(k)|^2$. Define the Leray multiplier by $P_0=I$ and $P_k=I-kk^{\\mathsf T}/|k|^2$ for $k\\ne0$. Then there is $T>0$, depending only on $d$, $s$, and $\\|u_0\\|_{H^s}$ and independent of $\\nu$ and all higher Sobolev norms of $u_0$, and a unique smooth real field $u:[0,T]\\times\\mathbb T^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ satisfying\n$$u(0,\\cdot)=u_0,\\qquad \\partial_tu+P((u\\cdot\\nabla)u)=\\nu\\Delta u,\\qquad \\operatorname{div}u=0.$$\nThe same time $T$ works for every integer Sobolev order $m\\ge s$, and $\\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}\\|u(t)\\|_{H^m}<\\infty$ for each such $m$. There is a unique smooth mean-zero scalar field $\\pi$ for which the equivalent full equation $\\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u=-\\nabla\\pi+\\nu\\Delta u$ holds.",
