          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be open, $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be open, $\\mathcal O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be the state domain of a conservation system, $n\\in\\mathbb R^d$ satisfy $|n|=1$, and $s\\in\\mathbb R$. The planar interface and its two open sides are\n$$\\Sigma=\\{(t,x)\\in I\\times\\Omega:n\\cdot x-st=0\\},\\qquad Q^-=\\{(t,x)\\in I\\times\\Omega:n\\cdot x-st<0\\},\\qquad Q^+=\\{(t,x)\\in I\\times\\Omega:n\\cdot x-st>0\\}.$$\nA state field is piecewise $C^1$ across $\\Sigma$ if on each side it is $C^1$ and, near every point of $\\Sigma$, each side field is the restriction of a $C^1$ map from a neighborhood in $I\\times\\Omega$ into $\\mathcal O$. The left and right traces $U_L,U_R:\\Sigma\\to\\mathcal O$ are the restrictions of those extensions to $\\Sigma$; they are independent of the chosen extensions because they agree with the same field on their respective open sides. For any state or flux quantity $G$ with these traces, define its jump by $[G]=G_R-G_L$. A planar shock profile is such a piecewise $C^1$ state field with its specified constant interface normal $n$ and speed $s$; the definition does not assert that it solves a conservation system.",
