          "statement": "For a one-dimensional system $U_t+\\partial_xF(U)=0$ with $F\\in C^1(\\mathcal O;\\mathbb R^m)$ on an open state domain $\\mathcal O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, the system is strictly hyperbolic at $V\\in\\mathcal O$ if the Jacobian $DF(V)$ has $m$ distinct real eigenvalues. Write them in increasing order as $\\lambda_1(V)<\\cdots<\\lambda_m(V)$. For states $U_L,U_R\\in\\mathcal O$ and speed $s\\in\\mathbb R$ satisfying the Rankine–Hugoniot equality\n$$F(U_R)-F(U_L)=s(U_R-U_L),$$\nassume strict hyperbolicity at both states. The jump is a $k$-Lax shock, $1\\leq k\\leq m$, if\n$$\\lambda_k(U_R)<s<\\lambda_k(U_L),$$\nand also $\\lambda_{k-1}(U_L)<s$ when $k>1$, and $s<\\lambda_{k+1}(U_R)$ when $k<m$. The outer inequality is omitted at each endpoint $k=1$ or $k=m$.",
