      "items": [
        {
          "id": "post-fd-boltzmann-model",
          "kind": "postulate",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Single-species elastic Boltzmann model",
          "dependency_level": 2,
          "deps": [
            "def-fd-elastic-collision-measure"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-fd-elastic-collision-measure": "mathematical-premise"
          },
          "physical_scope": "Adopted nonrelativistic, single-species, monatomic kinetic model in a three-dimensional periodic cell with elastic binary collision data and an incoming-pair factorization. Dilute-gas applicability is qualitative; no density threshold, hard-sphere or Boltzmann–Grad limit, many-particle derivation, existence assertion, or empirical validity is claimed.",
          "statement": "Let $\\Omega=\\prod_{j=1}^3(0,L_j)$, with $L_j>0$ measured in metres and opposite faces identified, and let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open time interval measured in seconds. The primitive state variable $f$ is a one-particle number-density field $f:I\\times\\Omega\\times\\mathbb R^3\\to[0,\\infty)$, measurable for the product Lebesgue sigma-algebra and periodic in $x$, where $x$ is in metres and velocity $v$ is in metres per second; $f\\,d^3x\\,d^3v$ counts particles, so $f$ has units $\\mathrm{s^3\\,m^{-6}}$. For solutions, require $f$ and its weak time and spatial derivatives $\\partial_t f$ and $\\nabla_x f$ to be represented locally by integrable functions, so $f$, $\\partial_t f$, and $v\\cdot\\nabla_x f$ are integrable on every compact subset of the product domain. Only translational kinetic energy $m|v|^2/2$ is modeled; no internal molecular modes are included. The single-particle mass $m>0$ in kilograms and Boltzmann constant $k_B>0$ in joules per kelvin are primitive fixed parameters of this formulation; neither is derived from the collision equation.\n\nAn elastic collision quadruple is $(v,w,v',w')\\in(\\mathbb R^3)^4$ satisfying $v+w=v'+w'$ and $|v|^2+|w|^2=|v'|^2+|w'|^2$; write $Q_{\\rm el}$ for this closed set and give it the Borel sets inherited from $(\\mathbb R^3)^4$. A collision-rate measure is a function $\\nu$ on those sets with $\\nu(\\varnothing)=0$, values in $[0,\\infty]$, and $\\nu(\\bigcup_n E_n)=\\sum_n\\nu(E_n)$ for pairwise disjoint measurable $E_n$. This nonnegative countably additive $\\nu$ is a primitive collision-rate input, invariant under each particle swap and under interchanging incoming and outgoing pairs, with rate normalization chosen so the collision operator has units of $f$ per second. For the collision equation, restrict each velocity section $f(x,\\cdot,t)$ to the collision domain $\\mathcal D_\\nu$ of positive finite-valued measurable densities; a nonnegative ambient state outside $\\mathcal D_\\nu$ is not assigned a collision operator by this postulate.\n\nAdopt one-sided incoming molecular chaos: at each collision point and time, the incoming two-particle velocity density used by the collision term is $f(x,v,t)f(x,w,t)$. No outgoing-pair factorization is adopted, and no derivation from reversible many-particle mechanics or propagation of chaos is asserted.\n\nFor $A=f(x,v,t)f(x,w,t)$ and $B=f(x,v',t)f(x,w',t)$, adopt a collision operator $Q_\\nu(f)$, with a jointly measurable locally integrable representative, whose weak action for every compact smooth velocity test and every moment or logarithmic test for which both sides converge absolutely is\n\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\psi(v)Q_\\nu(f)(x,v,t)\\,d^3v=\\frac14\\int_{Q_{\\rm el}}(B-A)\\bigl(\\psi(v)+\\psi(w)-\\psi(v')-\\psi(w')\\bigr)\\,d\\nu.$$\n\nThe adopted kinetic equation is\n\n$$\\partial_t f+v\\cdot\\nabla_x f=Q_\\nu(f)$$\n\nas an equality of locally integrable functions almost everywhere. These equations and the incoming factorization are physical model postulates, not deductions. This item asserts no empirical measurement, universal dilute-gas threshold, or solution existence.",
          "provenance": {
            "statement": "ai-altered",
            "proof": "not-applicable"
          },
          "sources": {
            "references": [
              {
                "title": "Mehran Kardar, MIT OpenCourseWare 8.333, Statistical Mechanics I, Lecture 8",
                "url": "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-333-statistical-mechanics-i-statistical-mechanics-of-particles-fall-2013/b8c07404633cdba3217e434ac06569ad_MIT8_333F13_Lec8.pdf",
                "locator": "Section III.D, printed pp. 49–54; the incoming molecular-chaos closure and its correlation limitation."
