                "title": "David Tong, Lectures on Kinetic Theory, Chapter 2",
                "url": "https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/tong/kintheory/two.pdf",
                "locator": "Sections 2.2–2.2.3; collision closure and detailed balance. Tong factorizes both pre-collision and post-collision pairs, so this treatment does not support the one-sided incoming factorization adopted here."
              }
            ]
          },
          "reference": "items/post-fd-boltzmann-model.md"
        },
        {
          "id": "def-fd-physical-kinetic-observables",
          "kind": "definition",
          "domain": "physics",
          "library": "physics",
          "title": "Kinetic number, mass and thermal observables",
          "dependency_level": 3,
          "deps": [
            "def-fd-kinetic-density",
            "post-fd-boltzmann-model"
          ],
          "dependency_roles": {
            "def-fd-kinetic-density": "mathematical-premise",
            "post-fd-boltzmann-model": "physical-assumption"
          },
          "physical_scope": "SI definitions for the single-species, three-dimensional, translational kinetic model on states with positive number density and, when temperature is used, positive centered second moment. No isotropy or empirical equation of state is assumed by the definitions.",
          "statement": "Work in three spatial and velocity dimensions, with $x$ in metres, $v$ in metres per second, and a nonnegative measurable one-particle number density $f(x,v,t)$ as defined by the kinetic-density model. Adopt Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ as in that definition, and put $v_*=1\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$ and $\\omega_3(v)=\\sum_{j=0}^3(|v|/v_*)^j$. Assume $\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}\\omega_3(v)f(x,v,t)\\,d^3v<\\infty$ at the state under consideration. Use the primitive fixed particle mass $m>0$ in kilograms and Boltzmann constant $k_B>0$ in joules per kelvin from the adopted Boltzmann model. The number density, mass density, mean velocity, peculiar velocity, translational temperature, stress, heat flux, and energy below are derived moment quantities. Where $n(x,t)>0$, define the number density, mass density, mean velocity, peculiar velocity, and translational temperature by\n\n$$n(x,t)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}f\\,d^3v,\\qquad \\rho(x,t)=m n(x,t),\\qquad u(x,t)=\\frac1{n(x,t)}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}v f\\,d^3v,\\qquad c(x,v,t)=v-u(x,t),$$\n\n$$T(x,t)=\\frac{m}{3k_B n(x,t)}\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|v-u(x,t)|^2 f(x,v,t)\\,d^3v.$$\n\nUse $u$ only where $n>0$, and use $T$ in the positive-temperature branch only where the centered second moment is strictly positive. Define the kinetic stress tensor, heat-flux vector, and total kinetic-energy density by\n\n$$P(x,t)=m\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}c\\otimes c\\,f\\,d^3v,\\qquad q(x,t)=\\frac m2\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|c|^2c\\,f\\,d^3v,\\qquad E(x,t)=\\frac m2\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}|v|^2f\\,d^3v.$$\n\nHere $n$ is in $\\mathrm{m^{-3}}$, $\\rho$ in $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^{-3}}$, $u$ in $\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}$, $T$ in kelvins, $P$ in pascals, $q$ in $\\mathrm{W\\,m^{-2}}$, and $E$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}}$. The definitions impose no isotropy on $P$; $p$ will denote a separate scalar pressure only in a specified local-equilibrium conclusion.\n\nLet $f_{\\rm ref}>0$ be a constant reference density with the same units as $f$. At $f=0$, define both $f\\log(f/f_{\\rm ref})$ and $f|\\log(f/f_{\\rm ref})|$ to be zero, their continuous extensions from $f>0$; their products with $v$ or $|v|$ are also zero there. Where both $\\int f|\\log(f/f_{\\rm ref})|\\,d^3v$ and $\\int |v|f|\\log(f/f_{\\rm ref})|\\,d^3v$ are finite, define the dimensionless-log kinetic entropy integral, its flux, the physical kinetic entropy density, and its flux by\n\n$$h=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}f\\log(f/f_{\\rm ref})\\,d^3v,\\qquad J_H=\\int_{\\mathbb R^3}v f\\log(f/f_{\\rm ref})\\,d^3v,\\qquad s_{\\rm kin}=-k_Bh,\\qquad j_{\\rm kin}=-k_BJ_H.$$\n\nThus $h$ and $J_H$ have units $\\mathrm{m^{-3}}$ and $\\mathrm{m^{-2}\\,s^{-1}}$, respectively; $s_{\\rm kin}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-3}\\,K^{-1}}$ and $j_{\\rm kin}$ has units $\\mathrm{J\\,m^{-2}\\,s^{-1}\\,K^{-1}}$. These are kinetic-model observables; $s_{\\rm kin}$ is not the fine-grained Gibbs entropy of a reversible many-particle state.",
