          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $n\\ge2$ and $p\\in(0,1)$. On the product probability space $\\{0,1\\}^n$ with independent Bernoulli$(p)$ coordinates $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$, define $\\bar X_n:=n^{-1}\\sum_{j=1}^nX_j$, $s_n^2:=(n-1)^{-1}\\sum_{j=1}^n(X_j-\\bar X_n)^2$, and $\\widehat{se}_n:=s_n/\\sqrt n$. The event that all coordinates are zero has probability $(1-p)^n>0$. On that event $\\bar X_n=s_n^2=\\widehat{se}_n=0$, although the target mean is $p>0$ and the population variance is $p(1-p)>0$, so the absolute error is $p$. This is an exact finite-sample probability-model example, not an experimental observation; it shows that a zero plug-in standard error in one realization does not establish zero population variance or zero estimation error.",
