          "kind": "proposition",
          "title": "Exact filtering for the declared linear Gaussian model",
          "statement": "Assume AC. Let $T\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$ and take an admissible stochastic coefficient model from the definition of the finite coefficient observation and innovation interface. Thus the coefficient states and observations satisfy $a_k=F_ka_{k-1}+q_k$ and $y_k=H_ka_k+r_k$, the initial state and all process and measurement noises are mutually independent Gaussian vectors with positive-semidefinite covariances, and every innovation covariance $S_k=H_kP^f_kH_k^T+R_k$ used by the recursion is positive definite. Run the finite-horizon Kalman recursion from the declared initial mean and covariance. Then, for each $0\\le k\\le T$, its Gaussian output is an everywhere-defined conditional-law version for the coefficient state $a_k$ given the observation history $y_{1:k}$. The Gaussian mean and covariance at each history are those of this chosen version. These values are defined even on sets of histories having zero probability under the model, although another version need not agree there. Initial, process, measurement, forecast-state, and analysis-state covariances may be singular; only the innovation covariances being solved must be positive definite. This is a deduction about the declared finite model, not a claim that a nonlinear or untruncated fluid equation is itself linear-Gaussian.",
