        {
          "id": "rem-fd-fluid-enkf-scope",
          "kind": "remark",
          "title": "EnKF large-ensemble scope and nonlinear limits",
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Choice (AC), meaning every family of nonempty sets has a choice function, for the Gaussian-law construction. Fix a nonnegative integer horizon $T\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$, positive integers $m_k$ for $0\\le k\\le T$ and $p_k$ for $1\\le k\\le T$, matrices $F_k\\in\\mathbb R^{m_k\\times m_{k-1}}$ and $H_k\\in\\mathbb R^{p_k\\times m_k}$ for $1\\le k\\le T$, and a finite linear-Gaussian coefficient model\n$$a_k=F_ka_{k-1}+q_k,\\qquad y_k=H_ka_k+r_k,$$\nwhere $\\mu_0\\in\\mathbb R^{m_0}$, $a_0\\sim N_{m_0}(\\mu_0,P_0)$, $q_k\\sim N_{m_k}(0,Q_k)$, and $r_k\\sim N_{p_k}(0,R_k)$ are mutually independent, $P_0,Q_k$ are positive semidefinite, and $R_k$ is positive definite. Fix arbitrary observation data $y_{1:T}$. For each integer ensemble size $N\\ge2$, draw the initial members $a_{N,i}^{a,0}$ independently from $N_{m_0}(\\mu_0,P_0)$. At each $k$, draw process perturbations $q_{N,i}^k\\sim N_{m_k}(0,Q_k)$ and observation perturbations $\\eta_{N,i}^k\\sim N_{p_k}(0,R_k)$, with all these perturbations mutually independent across members and times and independent of the initial ensemble, and set $a_{N,i}^{f,k}=F_ka_{N,i}^{a,k-1}+q_{N,i}^k$. Define $\\bar a_N^{f,k}=N^{-1}\\sum_i a_{N,i}^{f,k}$ and the divisor-$N$ covariance $C_N^{f,k}=N^{-1}\\sum_i(a_{N,i}^{f,k}-\\bar a_N^{f,k})(a_{N,i}^{f,k}-\\bar a_N^{f,k})^T$. The common gain and perturbed-observation update are\n$$K_N^k=C_N^{f,k}H_k^T(H_kC_N^{f,k}H_k^T+R_k)^{-1},\\qquad a_{N,i}^{a,k}=a_{N,i}^{f,k}+K_N^k(y_k+\\eta_{N,i}^k-H_ka_{N,i}^{f,k}).$$\nSince $C_N^{f,k}$ is positive semidefinite and $R_k$ is positive definite, this inverse exists. Define the analysis empirical mean and covariance by $\\bar a_N^{a,k}=N^{-1}\\sum_i a_{N,i}^{a,k}$ and $C_N^{a,k}=N^{-1}\\sum_i(a_{N,i}^{a,k}-\\bar a_N^{a,k})(a_{N,i}^{a,k}-\\bar a_N^{a,k})^T$. Let $m_{k|k}$ and $P_{k|k}$ be the mean and covariance produced by the exact Kalman recursion in the preceding filtering proposition, which defines an everywhere-defined Gaussian filtering law at every fixed history $y_{1:k}$. At each fixed $k\\le T$, $\\bar a_N^{a,k}$ and $C_N^{a,k}$ converge in probability to $m_{k|k}$ and $P_{k|k}$ as $N\\to\\infty$; moreover, for each fixed bounded Lipschitz function $\\varphi:\\mathbb R^{m_k}\\to\\mathbb R$, $N^{-1}\\sum_i\\varphi(a_{N,i}^{a,k})$ converges in probability to its integral under that filtering law. Here bounded Lipschitz means $\\sup_x|\\varphi(x)|<\\infty$ and $|\\varphi(x)-\\varphi(x')|\\le L\\|x-x'\\|_2$ for some finite $L$ and all $x,x'$. Convergence in probability means that for every $\\epsilon>0$, the probability that the error exceeds $\\epsilon$ tends to zero; use Euclidean norm for vectors, Frobenius norm for matrices, and absolute value for scalars. All probabilities refer to the ensemble sampling just described. The horizon is fixed as $N$ grows, with no uniform-in-time conclusion. At finite $N$, the common sample-covariance gain couples the update, and the theorem does not assert independent posterior samples or an exact posterior law for the ensemble. It covers the stated linear maps and perturbed-observation formula only. Covariance localization here means replacing the sample covariance by its entrywise product with a prescribed taper matrix; covariance inflation means rescaling the ensemble anomalies by a factor greater than one or adding a prescribed positive-semidefinite covariance; a transport update means applying a selected map to ensemble members instead of the displayed perturbed-observation formula. These modified procedures and nonlinear state or observation maps require their own statements and proofs. The claim is conditional on the chosen finite model; it is not a conclusion that a nonlinear fluid equation or an actual flow is described by it.",
