          "id": "prop-fd-finite-observation-identifiability",
          "kind": "proposition",
          "title": "Whitened observation identifiability and stability",
          "statement": "Let $E$ be a nonzero linear subspace of the finite coefficient space $\\mathbb R^m$, let $G:\\mathbb R^m\\to\\mathbb R^p$ be the declared linear observation map, and let $R\\in\\mathbb R^{p\\times p}$ be symmetric positive definite. Let $R^{1/2}$ be the unique symmetric positive-semidefinite square root of $R$ and set $W=(R^{1/2})^{-1}$, so $W^TW=R^{-1}$. The whitened map is $A=W(G|_E):E\\to\\mathbb R^p$, and define its smallest gain by $\\sigma_{\\min}(A)=\\inf\\{\\|Av\\|_2:v\\in E,\\ \\|v\\|_2=1\\}$; the singular-value decomposition shows this infimum is the smallest singular value and is attained. Identifiability on $E$ means injectivity of $G|_E$. It holds exactly when $\\sigma_{\\min}(A)>0$. If it holds, then for every true $a\\in E$, every realized error $e\\in\\mathbb R^p$, and datum $y=Ga+e$, the unique weighted least-squares estimate $\\hat a=\\arg\\min_{b\\in E}\\|W(y-Gb)\\|_2$ satisfies\n$$\\|\\hat a-a\\|_2\\le\\frac{\\|We\\|_2}{\\sigma_{\\min}(A)}.$$\nIf $\\sigma_{\\min}(A)=0$, a nonzero vector in $E\\cap\\ker G$ makes distinct coefficients observationally indistinguishable. The estimate is pointwise in the realized error; it is not a confidence or probability statement. For a pressure-field application, let $d\\ge1$ be an integer and let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$ be a nonempty spatial region, let $\\mathbb R^\\Omega$ be the set of real-valued functions on $\\Omega$, and let $\\Pi:E\\to\\mathbb R^\\Omega$ be the chosen linear pressure representation. An additive-constant coefficient direction is a nonzero $v_c\\in E$ for which $\\Pi(v_c)(x)=c$ for all $x\\in\\Omega$ and some nonzero $c\\in\\mathbb R$. If also $Gv_c=0$, then $G(a+t v_c)=Ga$ for every $a\\in E$ and $t\\in\\mathbb R$. Restricting the admissible coefficients to a linear complement of $\\operatorname{span}\\{v_c\\}$ fixes this direction; such a complement exists because $E$ is finite-dimensional. Alternatively, identify $a$ and $b$ exactly when $a-b\\in\\operatorname{span}\\{v_c\\}$ and treat each such equivalence class as one coefficient. This finite-dimensional result is not a general coefficient-identifiability theorem for a partial differential equation.",
