          "physical_scope": "Conditional energy identity for smooth periodic solutions of the adopted linear, constant-stratification rotating Boussinesq branch. It makes no claim about nonlinear or compressible flows, nonperiodic boundaries, the accuracy of the approximation, or empirical validity for an observed fluid.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the Lebesgue measure and energy-integration convention. Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be a nonempty open time interval, let $\\mathbb T^3=\\mathbb R^3/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^3$ have fundamental cell $Q_3=[0,2\\pi]^3$ with Lebesgue measure $\\lambda_3$, where $dx=d\\lambda_3(x)$ and time-space integration means the iterated Lebesgue integral with the spatial integral first, and represent fields by $2\\pi$-periodic lifts. Let $u:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be a velocity field and $b:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\to\\mathbb R$ a scalar buoyancy-acceleration field, each with continuous first time and spatial derivatives and continuous second spatial derivatives; let $\\pi:I\\times\\mathbb T^3\\to\\mathbb R$ be pressure per reference density with continuous first spatial derivatives. Let $f\\in\\mathbb R$, $N>0$, and $\\nu,\\kappa\\ge0$ be constants, with SI units s$^{-1}$, s$^{-1}$, m$^2$ s$^{-1}$, and m$^2$ s$^{-1}$, respectively. Assume these fields satisfy the linear rotating constant-stratification Boussinesq equations adopted in the geophysical-model postulate,\n\n$$u_t+f e_3\\times u+\\nabla\\pi=b e_3+\\nu\\Delta u,\\qquad \\nabla\\cdot u=0,\\qquad b_t+N^2u_3=\\kappa\\Delta b,$$\n\nwhere $e_3=(0,0,1)$, $e_3\\times u=(-u_2,u_1,0)$, $u_3$ is the vertical velocity component, $\\pi$ has units m$^2$ s$^{-2}$, $\\nabla q=(\\partial_{x_1}q,\\partial_{x_2}q,\\partial_{x_3}q)$, $\\nabla\\cdot w=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\partial_{x_j}w_j$, and $\\Delta q=\\sum_{j=1}^3\\partial_{x_j}^2q$, with $\\Delta$ applied componentwise to vector fields. Define the normalized energy density per unit constant reference density and its spatial integral by\n\n$$e(t,x):=\\frac12\\left(|u(t,x)|^2+\\frac{b(t,x)^2}{N^2}\\right),\\qquad E(t):=\\int_{Q_3}e(t,x)\\,dx,$$\n\nwhere $|u|^2=\\sum_{i=1}^3u_i^2$, $|\\nabla u|^2=\\sum_{i,j=1}^3(\\partial_{x_j}u_i)^2$, and $|\\nabla b|^2=\\sum_{j=1}^3(\\partial_{x_j}b)^2$. Then, for every $t_0<t_1$ in $I$,\n\n$$E(t_1)-E(t_0)=-\\int_{t_0}^{t_1}\\int_{Q_3}\\left(\\nu|\\nabla u|^2+\\frac{\\kappa}{N^2}|\\nabla b|^2\\right)\\,dx\\,dt.$$\n\nThus $E$ is conserved when $\\nu=\\kappa=0$ and is nonincreasing when $\\nu,\\kappa\\ge0$. In SI units, $e$ has units m$^2$ s$^{-2}$ and $E$ has units m$^5$ s$^{-2}$; multiplication by the constant reference density in kg m$^{-3}$ converts $E$ to joules. This is a conditional deduction from the adopted model and the stated smooth periodic hypotheses. It is not an approximation-error estimate, a nonlinear or compressible energy theorem, or evidence that the model describes an observed fluid.",
