          "physical_scope": "Pointwise algebraic consequence of the ideal rotating Boussinesq branch's Coriolis term in a right-handed local Cartesian model. No empirical motion or universal fluid behavior is asserted.",
          "empirical_premises": [],
          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "In the adopted rotating Boussinesq model, let $u=(u_1,u_2,u_3)\\in\\mathbb R^3$ be velocity in m s$^{-1}$, let $f\\in\\mathbb R$ be the constant Coriolis parameter in s$^{-1}$, and let $e_3=(0,0,1)$ be the upward unit vector in the right-handed Cartesian coordinates. The momentum equation places $+f e_3\\times u$ on its left side, so its signed acceleration contribution on the right side is $-f e_3\\times u$. Its contribution to the instantaneous rate of change of specific kinetic energy $|u|^2/2$ is the scalar product\n\n$$u\\cdot(-f e_3\\times u)=0.$$\n\nThus this ideal Coriolis term contributes zero specific power, in W kg$^{-1}$, at every point where the velocity is defined. This does not remove work by other forces or assert that an observed fluid follows the adopted model.",
