          "statement": "For an integer $d\\ge 1$, set $\\mathbb T^d:=\\mathbb R^d/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^d$ and let $Q_d:=[0,2\\pi]^d$ be its chosen fundamental cube. A scalar or vector field on $\\mathbb T^d$ is represented by its $(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^d$-periodic lift to $\\mathbb R^d$. For $k\\ge 0$ and $m\\ge 1$, $C^k_{\\rm per}(\\mathbb T^d;\\mathbb R^m)$ consists of such lifts whose componentwise partial derivatives through order $k$ exist, are continuous and periodic, and are bounded, uniformly continuous, and Riemann integrable on $Q_d$. For a scalar field $f$ and a vector field $v=(v_1,\\ldots,v_d)$, define\n\n$$\\nabla f:=(\\partial_1 f,\\ldots,\\partial_d f),\\qquad \\operatorname{div}v:=\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_jv_j,\\qquad \\Delta f:=\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_j^2f.$$\n\nThe integral of an integrable scalar field over $\\mathbb T^d$ means its Riemann integral over $Q_d$. For a matrix field $A=(A_{ij})$, $|A|^2:=\\sum_{i,j}|A_{ij}|^2$. The notation $C^1([0,T];C^k_{\\rm per})$ means that the field is continuously differentiable in time in the supremum norms of its spatial derivatives through order $k$. These are dimensionless mathematical conventions; they assign no physical units.",
