          "kind": "example",
          "title": "Constant phase is an energy-stationary state",
          "statement": "Let $d\\ge1$, $T>0$, $\\varepsilon,M,\\nu>0$, $W\\in C^2(\\mathbb R)$ be bounded below, and $c\\in\\mathbb R$. On $\\mathbb T^d=\\mathbb R^d/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^d$, consider the periodic equations $\\partial_t\\phi+u\\cdot\\nabla\\phi=M\\Delta\\mu_c$, $\\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla)u+\\nabla\\pi=\\nu\\Delta u-\\phi\\nabla\\mu_c$, and $\\nabla\\cdot u=0$, with $\\mu_c=\\varepsilon^{-1}W'(\\phi)-\\varepsilon\\Delta\\phi$. The constant fields $u=0$, $\\phi=c$, and $\\pi=0$ solve this system with constant chemical potential $\\mu_c=\\varepsilon^{-1}W'(c)$. Their total energy is $E(t)=(2\\pi)^d\\varepsilon^{-1}W(c)$ for all $t\\in[0,T]$, so this is an energy-stationary state, meaning that its energy is constant in time; no local-minimum or stability claim is made. All quantities are dimensionless mathematical variables.",
