          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $L>0$ metres, $U>0$ metres per second, $\\rho_*>0$ kilograms per cubic metre, $\\Phi_*>0$ in a chosen phase unit $[\\Phi]$, and $S>0$ dimensionless. Let $\\varepsilon,M,\\nu>0$ be dimensionless, set $\\beta=\\rho_*U^2/\\varepsilon$, $\\lambda_\\phi=\\rho_*U^2L^2\\varepsilon/\\Phi_*^2$, $\\eta=\\rho_*UL\\nu$, and $\\mathcal M=M\\Phi_*^2L/(\\rho_*U)$, and choose the dimensionless potential $W(q)=(q^2-1)^2/4$. Put $T_*=L/U$, $\\Omega_L=\\mathbb R^3/(2\\pi L\\mathbb Z)^3$, $f_b(\\varphi)=\\beta W(\\varphi/\\Phi_*)$, $\\mu=f_b'(\\varphi)-\\lambda_\\phi\\Delta_x\\varphi$, and $D_{ij}=(\\partial_{x_j}u_i+\\partial_{x_i}u_j)/2$.\n\n$$\\sigma=-pI_3+2\\eta D+\\left(f_b(\\varphi)-\\varphi\\mu+\\frac{\\lambda_\\phi}{2}|\\nabla_x\\varphi|^2\\right)I_3-\\lambda_\\phi\\nabla_x\\varphi\\otimes\\nabla_x\\varphi.$$\n\nOn this periodic domain, let $u$ be a velocity field in metres per second, $\\varphi$ a scalar phase field in unit $[\\Phi]$, and $p$ a scalar pressure in pascals. For a scalar field $g$, $\\nabla_xg=(\\partial_{x_1}g,\\partial_{x_2}g,\\partial_{x_3}g)$ and $\\Delta_xg=\\sum_j\\partial_{x_j}^2g$; for a vector field $u$, $\\nabla_x\\cdot u=\\sum_j\\partial_{x_j}u_j$; $I_3$ is the $3\\times3$ identity. Consider the adopted equations $\\partial_t\\varphi+u\\cdot\\nabla_x\\varphi=\\mathcal M\\Delta_x\\mu$, $\\nabla_x\\cdot u=0$, and $\\rho_*(\\partial_tu+(u\\cdot\\nabla_x)u)=\\operatorname{div}_x\\sigma$, where $\\sigma=-pI_3+2\\eta D+(f_b-\\varphi\\mu+\\lambda_\\phi|\\nabla_x\\varphi|^2/2)I_3-\\lambda_\\phi\\nabla_x\\varphi\\otimes\\nabla_x\\varphi$ and $(\\operatorname{div}_x\\sigma)_i=\\sum_j\\partial_{x_j}\\sigma_{ij}$. Define the kinetic-plus-phase free energy by $\\mathcal G(t)=\\int_{\\Omega_L}(\\rho_*|u|^2/2+f_b(\\varphi)+\\lambda_\\phi|\\nabla_x\\varphi|^2/2)\\,dx$. The constant fields $u=0$, $\\varphi=2\\Phi_*$, and $p=0$ satisfy these equations for all $t\\in[0,T_*S]$. Their chemical potential is the constant $\\mu=6\\beta/\\Phi_*$, and their kinetic-plus-phase free energy is $\\mathcal G(t)=(2\\pi L)^3\\beta W(2)=\\frac94(2\\pi L)^3\\beta<\\infty$. Thus this smooth model solution has normalized order parameter $\\varphi/\\Phi_*=2$ outside $[-1,1]$ while its free energy is finite and constant. The model's energy and equations do not impose that interval as a pointwise constraint; this example is not a maximum-principle theorem, stability claim, or reported observation.",
