          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice: for every sequence $(X_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$ of nonempty sets there is a function $f$ on $\\mathbb N$ such that $f(n)\\in X_n$. For $d\\in\\{1,2,3\\}$, let $Q_d=\\mathbb R^d/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^d$ carry the probability measure $d\\mu=(2\\pi)^{-d}dx$. For $k\\in\\mathbb Z^d$ define $e_k:Q_d\\to\\mathbb C$ by $e_k([x])=e^{ik\\cdot x}$; this is independent of the representative since $e^{2\\pi i k\\cdot n}=1$ for $n\\in\\mathbb Z^d$. For an integer $N\\ge1$ and a Borel-measurable map $q:Q_d\\to\\mathbb R^N$ with $\\int_{Q_d}|q(x)|\\,d\\mu(x)<\\infty$ in the Euclidean norm, considered up to equality $\\mu$-almost everywhere, define its componentwise Fourier coefficients by $\\widehat q(k)=\\int_{Q_d}q(x)e^{-ik\\cdot x}\\,d\\mu(x)$; this integral is finite componentwise because $|e_k(x)|=1$. The supplied space $H^0(Q_d;\\mathbb R^N)$ is the completion of finite real trigonometric polynomials in the norm $\\|q\\|_{H^0}^2=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z^d}|\\widehat q(k)|^2$, identified with its unique $L^2$ representative; real classes satisfy $\\widehat q(-k)=\\overline{\\widehat q(k)}$. For vector fields with these representatives, set $\\langle v,w\\rangle_2=\\int_{Q_d}v\\cdot w\\,d\\mu$. For an integer $r\\ge1$, an admissible finite solenoidal family consists of classes $v_1,\\ldots,v_r\\in H^0(Q_d;\\mathbb R^d)$ whose representatives are smooth $2\\pi$-periodic fields satisfying $\\operatorname{div}v_i=\\sum_{m=1}^d\\partial_{x_m}v_i^m=0$ and $\\langle v_i,v_j\\rangle_2=\\delta_{ij}$, where $\\delta_{ij}=1$ if $i=j$ and $0$ otherwise. Given such a family, a parameter $\\nu\\ge0$, and a coefficient vector $a=(a_1,\\ldots,a_r)\\in\\mathbb R^r$, put $u_a=\\sum_{i=1}^r a_i v_i$ and define\n\n$$A_{ij}=\\int_{Q_d}\\sum_{\\ell,m=1}^d(\\partial_{x_m}v_j^\\ell)(\\partial_{x_m}v_i^\\ell)\\,d\\mu,\\qquad c_{ijk}=\\int_{Q_d}\\sum_{\\ell,m=1}^d v_j^m(\\partial_{x_m}v_k^\\ell)v_i^\\ell\\,d\\mu.$$\n\nThe unforced finite solenoidal Galerkin coefficient dynamics is the initial-value system for $a:[0,T)\\to\\mathbb R^r$,\n\n$$a_i'(t)+\\sum_{j,k=1}^r c_{ijk}a_j(t)a_k(t)+\\nu\\sum_{j=1}^r A_{ij}a_j(t)=0,\\qquad a_i(0)=a_i^0,\\quad i=1,\\ldots,r,$$\n\nwhere $T\\in(0,\\infty]$ is the right endpoint of the interval on which a solution is defined and $a^0\\in\\mathbb R^r$ is the initial coefficient vector. A solution on $[0,T)$ is continuous on $[0,T)$ and continuously differentiable on $(0,T)$, has a right derivative at $0$, and its derivative extends continuously to $0$ with that value; the ODE holds for $0<t<T$ and at $t=0$ using this right derivative. When $T<\\infty$, no value or derivative at $T$ is required because $T$ is excluded; when $T=\\infty$, no endpoint condition is imposed at infinity. The displayed integrals are finite because their integrands are smooth on the compact torus. This is a finite-dimensional polynomial ODE; this definition asserts no convergence to a solution of the infinite-dimensional PDE.",
