          "statement": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $d\\in\\{2,3\\}$, $T>0$, and $Q_d=\\mathbb R^d/(2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^d$ with normalized volume $d\\mu=(2\\pi)^{-d}dx$. For a vector field $w$, use the componentwise Fourier coefficients $\\widehat w(k)=\\int_{Q_d}w(x)e^{-ik\\cdot x}\\,d\\mu(x)$ and norm $\\|w\\|_2=(\\int_{Q_d}|w(x)|^2\\,d\\mu(x))^{1/2}$. Define $P_0=I$ and, for $k\\ne0$, $P_k=I-kk^{\\mathsf T}/|k|^2$, with $\\widehat{Pw}(k)=P_k\\widehat w(k)$; $P$ is the Leray projector. For a smooth spatial vector field $w:Q_d\\to\\mathbb R^d$, define $\\operatorname{div}w(x)=\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_jw_j(x)$; for a smooth time-dependent vector field $w:[0,T]\\times Q_d\\to\\mathbb R^d$, define $\\operatorname{div}w(t,x)=\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_jw_j(t,x)$, with derivatives taken only in $x$; for a tensor field $F=(F_{ij})$, set $(\\operatorname{div}F)_i=\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_jF_{ij}$, and set $(v\\otimes v)_{ij}=v_i v_j$. Let $\\Delta=\\sum_{j=1}^d\\partial_j^2$. A smooth spatial field $w:Q_d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ is solenoidal, or divergence-free, iff $\\operatorname{div}w(x)=0$ for every $x\\in Q_d$; a smooth time-dependent field $w:[0,T]\\times Q_d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ is solenoidal iff $\\operatorname{div}w(t,x)=0$ for every $(t,x)\\in[0,T]\\times Q_d$. Given a constant $\\nu\\ge0$, smooth $f:[0,T]\\times Q_d\\to\\mathbb R^d$, a smooth solenoidal field $v:[0,T]\\times Q_d\\to\\mathbb R^d$, and a smooth solenoidal reference initial field $u_0:Q_d\\to\\mathbb R^d$, define the continuous residual and initial defect by\n\n$$r_v:=\\partial_t v+P\\operatorname{div}(v\\otimes v)-\\nu\\Delta v-Pf,\\qquad d_0:=\\|v(0,\\cdot)-u_0\\|_2.$$\n\nThe residual is a continuous function of time with values in $L^2(Q_d;\\mathbb R^d)$, so $\\int_0^T\\|r_v(t,\\cdot)\\|_2\\,dt$ is finite. This is a continuous-domain residual: finitely many collocation values do not by themselves bound this $L^1_tL^2_x$ quantity. All quantities here are mathematical; no physical units are assigned to $\\nu$.",
