          "statement": "Let $(\\mathcal Z,\\Sigma)$ be a measurable space and let $\\mu$ be a probability measure on it, meaning a countably additive measure on $\\Sigma$ with $\\mu(\\mathcal Z)=1$. Let $X$ be a real normed vector space and give it the norm-Borel $\\sigma$-algebra generated by its norm-open sets. Let $S,\\widetilde S:\\mathcal Z\\to X$ be measurable maps. Let $J:X\\to\\mathbb R$ be $L$-Lipschitz, meaning $|J(x)-J(y)|\\le L\\|x-y\\|_X$ for all $x,y\\in X$, with $L\\ge0$. Suppose\n\n$$\\int_{\\mathcal Z}\\bigl(\\|S\\|_X^2+\\|\\widetilde S\\|_X^2\\bigr)\\,d\\mu<\\infty,\\qquad \\int_{\\mathcal Z}\\|S-\\widetilde S\\|_X^2\\,d\\mu\\le\\varepsilon^2$$\n\nfor some finite $\\varepsilon\\ge0$. For a measurable scalar function $h$, write $\\|h\\|_{L^2(\\mu)}=(\\int_{\\mathcal Z}|h|^2\\,d\\mu)^{1/2}$. Let $g:\\mathcal Z\\to[0,\\infty)$ be measurable with $\\int g\\,d\\mu=1$ and $g\\le C$ $\\mu$-almost everywhere for a finite $C\\ge1$, and define the target probability law by $\\nu(E)=\\int_Eg\\,d\\mu$ for $E\\in\\Sigma$, with target expectation $\\mathbb E_\\nu[H]=\\int Hg\\,d\\mu$ whenever the integral exists. Then the measurable scalar observables $J\\circ S$ and $J\\circ\\widetilde S$ are $\\nu$-integrable and\n\n$$\\left|\\mathbb E_\\nu[J(S)]-\\mathbb E_\\nu[J(\\widetilde S)]\\right|\\le L\\sqrt C\\,\\varepsilon.$$\n\nThe estimate assumes a population mean-square error under $\\mu$ and a bounded target density $g$; a finite test sample by itself establishes neither assumption. The state space may represent fluid fields when that measurable state model is supplied. For a physical application, both observables must use the same declared scalar unit; this mathematical proposition assigns no physical units.",
