          "inventory_expansion_required": false,
          "statement": "Let $d_s=2$, $D=(0,1)^2$, and $I=[0,1]$. Let $X$ consist of the constant product-measurable fields $c e_1:D\\times I\\to\\mathbb R^2$, where $c\\in\\mathbb R$ and $e_1=(1,0)$, with norm $\\|c e_1\\|_X=|c|$ and its Borel $\\sigma$-algebra; thus $X$ is isometric to $\\mathbb R$ and is a separable real Banach space. Let $Z=\\{0,1\\}$ with its power-set $\\sigma$-algebra and define $S(z)=0$, $\\widetilde S(0)=0$, $\\widetilde S(1)=M e_1$ for arbitrary $M>0$, and $J(c e_1)=c$, which is $1$-Lipschitz. An $X$-valued simple map means a map with finite range and measurable fibers. A map is strongly measurable with respect to a probability law if, outside a measurable set of measure zero, it is a pointwise norm limit of a sequence of simple maps. Here $S$ and $\\widetilde S$ are themselves simple maps, so their constant approximating sequences establish strong measurability for either law below. For $z\\in Z$, define the Dirac law $\\delta_z$ by $\\delta_z(E)=1$ if $z\\in E$ and $0$ otherwise. Take training law $\\mu=\\delta_0$ and target law $\\nu=\\delta_1$. Then the training-law mean-square state error is zero, while the target-law observable bias is $M$. Absolute continuity $\\nu\\ll\\mu$ means that $\\mu(E)=0$ implies $\\nu(E)=0$ for every measurable $E\\subseteq Z$. The target law is not absolutely continuous with respect to the training law, so the bounded-density hypothesis of the shift certificate fails; as $M$ is arbitrary, training-law error alone supplies no finite uniform target-bias bound.",
