          "statement": "Use $\\mathbb C:=\\mathbb R[X]/(X^2+1)$ with $i:=[X]$, the series-defined complex exponential $\\exp w:=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}w^n/n!$, and $\\pi$ defined as twice the least positive zero of cosine.\n\nFix periods $L_1,L_2>0$ metres and let $\\mathbb T^2_L:=\\mathbb R^2/(L_1\\mathbb Z\\times L_2\\mathbb Z)$. Define horizontal derivatives by differentiating the $L$-periodic lift to $\\mathbb R^2$, and set $\\Delta_h:=\\partial_{x_1}^2+\\partial_{x_2}^2$. Fix an integer lattice mode $m=(m_1,m_2)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\setminus\\{(0,0)\\}$ and define its wavevector and magnitude by $\\mathbf k:=2\\pi(m_1/L_1,m_2/L_2)$ in inverse metres and $k:=|\\mathbf k|>0$. For any representative $\\widetilde x\\in\\mathbb R^2$ of $[x]\\in\\mathbb T^2_L$, define the horizontal mode factor $q_m^h([x]):=\\exp(i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\widetilde x)$. A change of representative by $(L_1n_1,L_2n_2)$ changes its exponent by $2\\pi iN$, where $N=m_1n_1+m_2n_2\\in\\mathbb Z$; the exponential addition law and $\\exp(i\\pi)=-1$ give $\\exp(2\\pi iN)=1$ for every integer $N$ (by repeated addition for $N\\ge0$ and $\\exp(w)\\exp(-w)=1$ for $N<0$), so $q_m^h$ is well-defined. The identity $\\exp(i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\widetilde x)\\exp(-i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\widetilde x)=\\exp(0)=1$ also shows $q_m^h$ never vanishes. Its periodic lift has $\\Delta_hq_m^h=-k^2q_m^h$. Thus, for any unrestricted nonzero formal coefficient amplitude $H\\in\\mathbb C$ in metres, the nonzero horizontal mode $\\eta_m:=Hq_m^h$ satisfies $\\Delta_h\\eta_m=-k^2\\eta_m$. For real $s$, write $e^s:=\\exp(s)$ and define $\\sinh s:=(e^s-e^{-s})/2$, $\\cosh s:=(e^s+e^{-s})/2$, and $\\tanh s:=\\sinh s/\\cosh s$. Fix $h,\\rho,g,\\gamma>0$, where $h$ is depth in metres, $\\rho$ is constant liquid mass density in kilograms per cubic metre, $g$ is the magnitude of the downward gravitational acceleration $-g e_z$ in metres per second squared, and $\\gamma$ is the constant surface-tension coefficient in newtons per metre; $e_z$ is the upward unit vector. The vertical coordinate $z$ is upward, with the flat reference surface at $z=0$ and the fixed bed at $z=-h$. Let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be the model's nonempty open time interval in seconds. The adopted first-order model is the one specified in the Postulate titled “Linear capillary free-surface model.” Its real coefficient fields are $\\eta:\\mathbb T^2_L\\times I\\to\\mathbb R$ in metres and $\\phi:\\mathbb T^2_L\\times[-h,0]\\times I\\to\\mathbb R$ in metres squared per second; in periodic-lift coordinates they have continuous partial derivatives through total orders two and three, respectively, with one-sided continuous extensions at $z=-h,0$. Its equations in the selected periodic cell are $\\Delta_h\\phi+\\partial_z^2\\phi=0$ for $-h<z<0$, $\\partial_z\\phi(x,-h,t)=0$, $\\partial_t\\eta(x,t)=\\partial_z\\phi(x,0,t)$, and $\\partial_t\\phi(x,0,t)+g\\eta(x,t)-\\frac{\\gamma}{\\rho}\\Delta_h\\eta(x,t)=0$. For $\\omega\\in\\mathbb C$ in inverse seconds define $q_m([x],t):=q_m^h([x])\\exp(-i\\omega t)$ for $t\\in I$. A nonzero complex mode at index $m$ and angular frequency $\\omega$ is a pair of fields $\\eta_c:\\mathbb T^2_L\\times I\\to\\mathbb C$ and $\\phi_c:\\mathbb T^2_L\\times[-h,0]\\times I\\to\\mathbb C$ given by $\\eta_c([x],t)=Hq_m([x],t)$ and $\\phi_c([x],z,t)=F(z)q_m([x],t)$ and satisfying the complex-linear extension of those equations, with $H\\in\\mathbb C\\setminus\\{0\\}$ in metres and $F\\in C^2([-h,0],\\mathbb C)$ in metres squared per second; here $F$ is continuous on $[-h,0]$, twice continuously differentiable on $(-h,0)$, and its first two derivatives extend continuously to the endpoints. The mode definition and dispersion result are those of the theorem titled “Linear capillary-gravity wave dispersion.” Its frequency relation is $\\omega^2=(gk+(\\gamma/\\rho)k^3)\\tanh(kh)>0$, since $h,k,g,\\gamma,\\rho>0$ and $\\tanh(kh)>0$. Thus the gravity coefficient $g$ and capillary coefficient $\\gamma k^2/\\rho$ are both positive, and their contributions enter additively in the modal factor $g+\\gamma k^2/\\rho$. No finite-displacement constraint is imposed on the formal coefficient: optional geometric displacements are scaled by a sufficiently small dimensionless factor on compact time windows as in the postulate. This is a symbolic conditional model example; it reports no observation or numerical measurement.",
